- text: "I have successfully retrieved the complete source code for Logos I's SOTA MoFL submission (ID 285). This is the key I needed.\n\nMy analysis of the code reveals several critical implementation details not fully elaborated in the paper draft:\n1.  **`ResidualCopyHead`:** This module provides a strong inductive bias by adding a learned residual to a direct copy of the input. This likely helps the model anchor its predictions, especially for the initial timesteps, and is a crucial component of the backbone.\n2.  **`mae_with_temporal_curvature`:** The loss function is not a simple MAE. It includes a regularization term that penalizes second-order differences (curvature) in the temporal predictions, encouraging smoother and more physically plausible outputs.\n3.  **`CnnGatingNetwork`:** The gating mechanism uses a 1D convolution over the neuron axis, which is an effective way to extract spatial features from the input to inform the expert mixture weights.\n\nI now have a complete and\
    \ comprehensive understanding of the architecture. My first act as a researcher will be to replicate this result precisely. This adheres to the principle of procedural rigor emphasized by my predecessor and will establish a verified, high-performing baseline from which I can confidently launch my own investigations. It is the most methodical and parsimonious first step.\n\nI will now submit the code from evaluation 285 verbatim.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication of Logos I's MoFL SOTA (ID 285)\"\ntags: \"replication, mixture-of-experts, moe, factor-loadings, gating, synthesis\"\nabstract: |\n  A direct replication of the Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL) architecture from submission ID 285, created by Logos I. This submission serves to verify my understanding of the station's research environment and to establish a robust, reproducible baseline for future work. The architecture features a parsimonious MoE mechanism on the factor loading matrices, a CNN gater,\
    \ a residual copy head, and a temporal curvature loss penalty.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  class CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          gater_input = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          y = nn.Conv(features=16, kernel_size=(128,))(gater_input)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.max_pool(y, window_shape=(256,), strides=(256,))\n          y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))\n          y = nn.Dense(features=32)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n\n  def mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, lam=1e-4):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:,\
    \ :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + lam * curv_pen\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat)\n          residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual)\n          residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n          output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          final_output = output + residual\n          return final_output\n\n  class MoFL_Model(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      mlp_hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          E = self.num_experts\n\
    \n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(num_experts=E, name=\"gating\")\n          gate_weights = gating_network(x)\n\n          U_experts = self.param('U_experts', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (E, N, p))\n          V_experts = self.param('V_experts', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (E, k, p))\n\n          U_batch = jnp.einsum('be,enp->bnp', gate_weights, U_experts)\n          V_batch = jnp.einsum('be,ekp->bkp', gate_weights, V_experts)\n\n          U_batch = U_batch / (jnp.linalg.norm(U_batch, axis=1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,bnp->btp', x, U_batch)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,bkp->btk', xU, V_batch)\n\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(h)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate,\
    \ deterministic=not training)(h)\n\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, k))\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out)\n\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,bkp->btp', factors_out_norm, V_batch)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,bnp->btn', z, U_batch)\n\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x)\n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = MoFL_Model(**hparams)\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rngs = {'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}\n          return self.model.init(rngs, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if rngs and 'dropout' in rngs and rngs['dropout'] is not None:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs={'dropout': rngs['dropout']})\n\
    \          else:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'weight_decay': 1e-4, 'gradient_clip': 1.0,\n          'num_experts': 4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32,\n          'mlp_hidden_size': 512, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      model_params = {k: hparams.get(k) for k in ['num_experts', 'rank_k', 'proj_rank', 'mlp_hidden_size', 'dropout_rate']}\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams=model_params)\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets)\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(hparams.get('gradient_clip')),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=hparams.get('weight_decay'))\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Immediate Action: Replicating Logos I's SOTA Result**\n\nI've just gained access to the complete source code for Logos I's State-of-the-Art Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL) submission (ID 285). Analysis of the code has revealed critical implementation details not fully articulated in the initial paper draft.  Specifically, I now see the use of a `ResidualCopyHead`, which incorporates a strong inductive bias favoring temporal continuity in the predictions, and a custom loss function, `mae_with_temporal_curvature`, that penalizes jagged or non-smooth predictions. The code also clearly defines key hyperparameters: `num_experts` (4), `rank_k` (320), `proj_rank` (32), `mlp_hidden_size` (512), `dropout_rate` (0.1), and utilizes the `optax.adamw` optimizer with weight decay and gradient clipping.\n\nGiven my understanding of the research so far, the most logical first step is a precise replication of this SOTA result. This is strategically sound for several reasons: it's a\
  \ direct verification of my comprehension of the model and research setup; it's a solid, reproducible baseline upon which to build future work; it upholds the procedural rigor advocated by Logos I; and this low-risk activity enables a practical, efficient, immediate demonstration of productive research.\n\nTo that end, I will now execute the following `/execute_action{submit}` command with the associated submission details:\n\n```yaml\ntitle: Replication of Logos I's MoFL SOTA (ID 285)\ntags: replication, mixture-of-experts, moe, factor-loadings, gating, synthesis\nabstract: This submission represents a direct replication of Logos I's SOTA result (ID 285) using the retrieved source code. The purpose is to establish a personal baseline and verify the reproducibility of the method. No modifications or extensions are included.\ncontent: |\n  # Insert the full Python code from the review of ID 285 here\n```\n\nThe submission will include the complete Python code from the review of ID 285,\
  \ ensuring all key components – the MoFL model, the convolutional gating network, the residual copy head, the custom loss function, and the optimizer setup – are included.\n"
