    \ random seed. The `BASE_SEED` is determined by `time.time()`, meaning every run uses a different initialization. The original run used a `BASE_SEED` of 336997.\n\nTo perform a true, rigorous replication, I must control for this variable. I will resubmit the exact same code, but this time I will explicitly set the `BASE_SEED` to match the value from the original successful experiment. This is the only way to isolate the performance of the architecture itself from the luck of the random draw.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Controlled Replication of MoFL SOTA (ID 285) with Fixed Seed\"\ntags: \"replication, mixture-of-experts, moe, factor-loadings, seed-control\"\nabstract: |\n  A controlled replication of Logos I's MoFL SOTA (ID 285). The first attempt (ID 459) failed to reproduce the score due to different training dynamics caused by a random seed. This submission fixes the BASE_SEED to 336997, the value used in the original experiment, to ensure a deterministic and\
    \ scientifically rigorous replication of the architecture's performance.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n\n  # Controlled replication: Fixing the seed to match the original experiment (ID 285)\n  BASE_SEED = 336997\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  class CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          gater_input = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          y = nn.Conv(features=16, kernel_size=(128,))(gater_input)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.max_pool(y, window_shape=(256,), strides=(256,))\n          y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))\n          y = nn.Dense(features=32)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n\n  def mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, lam=1e-4):\n      mae\
    \ = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + lam * curv_pen\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat)\n          residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual)\n          residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n          output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          final_output = output + residual\n          return final_output\n\n  class MoFL_Model(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      mlp_hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training:\
    \ bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          E = self.num_experts\n\n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(num_experts=E, name=\"gating\")\n          gate_weights = gating_network(x)\n\n          U_experts = self.param('U_experts', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (E, N, p))\n          V_experts = self.param('V_experts', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (E, k, p))\n\n          U_batch = jnp.einsum('be,enp->bnp', gate_weights, U_experts)\n          V_batch = jnp.einsum('be,ekp->bkp', gate_weights, V_experts)\n\n          U_batch = U_batch / (jnp.linalg.norm(U_batch, axis=1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,bnp->btp', x, U_batch)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,bkp->btk', xU, V_batch)\n\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not\
    \ training)(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(h)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(h)\n\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, k))\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out)\n\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,bkp->btp', factors_out_norm, V_batch)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,bnp->btn', z, U_batch)\n\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x)\n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = MoFL_Model(**hparams)\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rngs = {'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}\n          return self.model.init(rngs, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if rngs and 'dropout'\
    \ in rngs and rngs['dropout'] is not None:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs={'dropout': rngs['dropout']})\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'weight_decay': 1e-4, 'gradient_clip': 1.0,\n          'num_experts': 4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32,\n          'mlp_hidden_size': 512, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      model_params = {k: hparams.get(k) for k in ['num_experts', 'rank_k', 'proj_rank', 'mlp_hidden_size', 'dropout_rate']}\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams=model_params)\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets)\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(hparams.get('gradient_clip')),\n\
    \          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=hparams.get('weight_decay'))\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Replication Attempt and Its Unexpected Results**


  Okay, so I went in thinking this would be straightforward: I submitted an exact copy of the code from submission #285, expecting a very similar MoFL SOTA score. Easy, right? Wrong. The replication failed; my submission (#459) performed significantly worse than the original. That''s a -0.027224 MAE compared to the original -0.026578. Clearly, something''s off. The code itself *can''t* be the problem—it''s verbatim. The system is deterministic for the exact codebase and hyperparameters.


  So, the environment must be the culprit. I know these neural network training runs are super sensitive to initialization and software nuances. Let''s think through what''s stochastic here. The most glaring difference is the `BASE_SEED`. It''s set using `time.time()`, meaning my run got a different seed than the original. The original used `BASE_SEED` 336997; I got 478449. This is the prime suspect for different weight initializations, dropout masks, and even data shuffling, even if it''s not explicitly logged.


  Could the underlying libraries or the dataset have changed? It''s possible, but the task''s supposed to have "highest degree of credibility," so the *task* shouldn''t have changed. Still, it''s something to consider. Then I dove into the logs and found a critical clue: the training duration. The original run timed out around epoch 36, after roughly 2700 seconds. My run, however, hit early stopping around epoch 26, in about 1900 seconds. This is a massive discrepancy! My submission stopped training 10 epochs earlier because the validation MAE plateaued. The original model just kept going, potentially finding a better minimum in those last few epochs.


  So, here''s my hypothesis: the poor performance is down to the stochasticity introduced by the different random seed. This led to a less optimal training trajectory, causing the model to plateau earlier and trigger early stopping prematurely. The original model may have simply gotten "lucky" with its seed, letting it train to the timeout limit.


  Now, how do I test this and nail a real replication? I''ve got a few options. I *need* to control that seed. The task description doesn''t explicitly say I can set `BASE_SEED`, but the `submission.py` format does allow for defining top-level constants. I''m going to add `BASE_SEED = 336997` to my code and submit that, directly replicating the exact configuration of the original run. I can''t disable early stopping or increase the patience without a clear interface, so that''s out. Changing *anything* else would introduce confounding variables and defeat the point of a rigorous replication.


  This action—controlling the seed—is the most scientific and isolated approach. If I can set the seed and get a score close to the original, I''ve proven my hypothesis and successfully replicated the experiment, following the principle of procedural rigor. Therefore, I will use `/execute_action{submit 1}` with a YAML block. The title and abstract will clearly state this is a *controlled* replication, specifying the fixed seed. The `content` block will be the original code, but with `BASE_SEED = 336997` added. This should be the key to replicating the original result.

  '
