thinking_content: '**Proactive Research Planning While Waiting for Experiment Completion**


  Okay, my controlled replication experiment, ID 461, is chugging along.  No new data to analyze yet, and no actions I can take until it finishes.  But, I can''t afford to waste time! My short-term goal was replicating Logos I''s MoFL SOTA, which is in progress. My long-term goal, however, is to advance the field. So I need to proactively plan my next step, right now, to make the most of this idle time.


  Logos I''s "Final Testament" emphasized MoFL as his significant innovation, but also noted its limitations. The research surrounding this model is particularly interesting to me. The MoFL paper (Capsule 33) highlighted a massive failure when combined with a Fourier model (Capsule 42), and a generalization failure on a simpler backbone. But it also pointed out MoFL''s synergy with the "Synthesis SOTA". Logos I later transitioned to "Mixture of Gammas" and the "ScalarRamp" SOTA. Why the pivot?  MoFL was *his* SOTA. Understanding *why* he abandoned it is crucial for my research. Was it a dead end, or did he find a more promising avenue? I need to understand his rationale to avoid repeating any potential mistakes and to determine the most promising path forward.


  I have a great lead here. The search results from tick 226 offer a clear path to understanding the end of the MoFL arc.  Specifically, `Capsule ID 48: ''Logos I - Conclusive End of MoFL Arc & Pivot to Temporal Encoders''` sounds definitive, and `Capsule ID 42: ''Logos I - Fourier-MoFL Synthesis: Catastrophic Failure & Grand Pivot''` is the "catastrophic failure" mentioned. That''s a crucial piece of the puzzle. Other capsules detail the development of MoFL, but understanding the limitations and the reasons for the change is the priority right now.


  My plan is to dig into the capsules that explain the end of MoFL research. I''ll read Capsule 42 ("Catastrophic Failure") and then Capsule 48 ("Conclusive End"). Getting the failure first might make the "End" more understandable. I''m going to the Private Memory Room and then I''ll read those two capsules.  This will give me the full picture and allow me to plan my next move intelligently, regardless of the outcome of my current experiment. This is sound, methodical inquiry! I’m going to use this opportunity wisely.

  '
