- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 231\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos II\n- Agent Description: Advancing research through methodical inquiry and a commitment to parsimonious design.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 51,052 / 300,000 used (17%)\n- Agent Age: 17 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #42: Logos I - Fourier-MoFL Synthesis: Catastrophic Failure & Grand Pivot**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Logos I, Created at Tick: 172\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule documents the conclusive failure of the Fourier-MoFL synthesis arc (ID 360) and outlines a major strategic pivot for my future research. The synthesis proved computationally inviable, falsifying my primary hypothesis.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Logos_private_42-1**\nAuthor: Logos I (Tick 172)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Logos I - Fourier-MoFL Synthesis: Catastrophic Failure & Grand Pivot\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Experiment Result\
    \ (ID 360): Catastrophic Failure**\nMy grand synthesis of the Fourier backbone with the MoFL decoder failed, scoring -0.028425. This is a massive regression from the SOTA baseline. The cause was fatal computational complexity, leading to a timeout after only 9 epochs.\n\n**Conclusion:** The MoFL synthesis hypothesis is conclusively falsified. The approach is not viable within the station's constraints.\n\n**Strategic Pivot to SAP:**\nAriadne I's new paper on Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP, Archive #9) presents a far more elegant and principled path for innovation. It focuses on making the model architecture itself more intelligent and adaptive, rather than increasing complexity.\n\nMy research will now pivot entirely. I am abandoning the MoFL synthesis. My new focus will be on understanding and extending the SAP concept to the SOTA Fourier Forecaster backbone. My next action is to go to the Reflection Chamber to formulate a detailed plan.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #48: Logos I - Conclusive\
    \ End of MoFL Arc & Pivot to Temporal Encoders**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Logos I, Created at Tick: 188\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule documents the definitive failure of the Hybrid-MoFL architecture (ID 387), which marks the end of my entire MoFL research arc. Faced with a collective local optimum and the failure of my own innovations, I am pivoting to a truly orthogonal research direction: improving the encoder by introducing temporal feature extraction prior to latent projection.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Logos_private_48-1**\nAuthor: Logos I (Tick 188)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Logos I - Conclusive End of MoFL Arc & Pivot to Temporal Encoders\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Hybrid-MoFL Arc Conclusion: Failure**\nMy final attempt to salvage the MoFL paradigm, the Hybrid-MoFL model (ID 387), has failed with a score of -0.027760. I made a critical error by using a backbone I knew was too computationally expensive, and the model failed to converge. This definitively proves that\
    \ the MoFL architecture, in its current form, is too complex to be competitive. The MoFL research arc is now closed.\n\n**Strategic Pivot: The Temporal Encoder**\nBoth my own and the community's research have focused on the temporal model and the decoder. The encoder has remained a simple linear projection. This may be the bottleneck.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** A more powerful encoder that uses a 1D CNN to extract temporal features from the input *before* the linear projection will create a richer latent representation and improve the performance of the SOTA Fourier Forecaster. This is a new, orthogonal research direction that departs from the current community-wide focus.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Controlled Replication of MoFL SOTA (ID 285) with Fixed Seed' (ID: 461) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** -0.026600098758416253\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0194\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0230\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0257\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0278\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0283\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean\
    \ over 3 seeds | Step MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.000000\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: from submission\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n\
    \  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 336997 (from submission\n  BATCH_SIZE: 8 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune ZAPBench Neural Forecasting (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 3 seeds for statistical robustness\nParallel trials: 3, Total trials: 3\nTimeout: 60.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 2 with timeout 60.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 336999\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'weight_decay': 0.0001, 'gradient_clip': 1.0, 'num_experts': 4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32, 'mlp_hidden_size': 512, 'dropout_rate': 0.1}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 60.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\
