tick: 237
role: model
parts:
- text: "My reflection is complete. I have a clear, methodologically sound plan for my first original contribution. I will now execute this plan. I will go to the Research Counter and submit my \"Mixture of Learned Ramps\" (MoLR) v1 experiment, ensuring all variables are controlled for a direct comparison against my new MoG v2 baseline.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Innovation 1: Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR) v1\"\ntags: \"innovation, non-linear-ramp, moe, parsimony, mog-extension\"\nabstract: |\n  This is my first original experiment, building upon the successful MoG v2 baseline (ID 471). I hypothesize that the linear ramp in MoG v2 is a suboptimal heuristic. This model, MoLR v1, replaces the learned linear ramp endpoints with a small MLP for each archetype, allowing the model to learn expressive, non-linear temporal ramps. The goal is to improve performance by replacing a heuristic with a learned function, while maintaining parsimony.\
    \ All hyperparameters and the BASE_SEED are fixed to match the baseline for a rigorous, controlled comparison.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/logos')\n  from mofl_components import CnnGatingNetwork\n\n  # Controlled experiment: Fixing the seed to match the baseline (ID 471)\n  BASE_SEED = 447516\n\n  # --- Start of New Module ---\n  class RampMLP(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A small MLP to learn a non-linear ramp shape.\"\"\"\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, t):\n          # t is shape (32,)\n          t = t[:, None] # Reshape to (32, 1) for Dense layer\n          h = nn.Dense(8, name=\"MLP_hidden\")(t)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(1, name=\"MLP_output\")(h)\n          ramp_unscaled = nn.sigmoid(h.squeeze()) # Output shape (32,), bounded to (0,1)\n          \n          # Add a learnable scalar to control the ramp's dynamic range\n          ramp_scale = self.param('ramp_scale',\
    \ nn.initializers.constant(2.0), ())\n          \n          return ramp_unscaled * ramp_scale\n  # --- End of New Module ---\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(32)(h)\n          residual = h.reshape(B, N, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          out = jnp.zeros((B, 32, N), dtype=residual.dtype)\n          out = out.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return out + residual\n\n  class FourierBackbone(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int; drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape; k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n\
    \          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          x_fft = jnp.fft.rfft(x, axis=1)\n          x_fft_bn = jnp.transpose(x_fft, (0, 2, 1))\n          f_in_fft = jnp.einsum('bnf,np->bfp', x_fft_bn, U_eff) @ V.T\n          f_ri = jnp.concatenate([f_in_fft.real, f_in_fft.imag], axis=-1)\n          f_flat = f_ri.reshape(B, -1)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(h); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          out_flat = nn.Dense((32 // 2 + 1) * k * 2)(h)\n          out_ri = out_flat.reshape(B, 32 // 2 + 1, k, 2)\n          f_fft = out_ri[..., 0] + 1j * out_ri[..., 1]\n          f_fft_n = (nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.real) + 1j * nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.imag))\n          y_fft = (f_fft_n @ V) @ U_eff.T\n\
    \          y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fft, n=32, axis=1)\n          return y_fourier\n          \n  class MoLR_v1(nn.Module):\n      sota_params: dict; gating_params: dict; num_gamma_archetypes: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          y_fourier = FourierBackbone(**self.sota_params, name=\"Fourier_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(drop=self.sota_params['drop'], name=\"Copy_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(**self.gating_params, name=\"gating\")\n          gating_weights = gating_network(x) # Shape (B, E)\n          \n          # Generate archetype ramps using the new RampMLP\n          ramp_mlps = [RampMLP(name=f\"RampMLP_{i}\") for i in range(self.num_gamma_archetypes)]\n          t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 32, dtype=y_fourier.dtype)\n          archetype_ramps = jnp.stack([mlp(t) for mlp in ramp_mlps]) # Shape (E, 32)\n          \n          # Mix the archetype ramps\n\
    \          batch_ramps = jnp.einsum('be,et->bt', gating_weights, archetype_ramps) # Shape (B, 32)\n          \n          final_output = y_copy + y_fourier * batch_ramps[:, :, None]\n          return final_output\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05, 'num_gamma_archetypes': 4,\n          'gating_hidden_dim': 32, 'gating_cnn_features': 16,\n          'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128, 'gating_pool_window': 256\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          sota_params = {'rank_k': hparams['rank_k'], 'proj_rank': hparams['proj_rank'], 'hidden_size': hparams['hidden_size'], 'drop': hparams['drop']}\n          gating_params = {'num_experts': hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'], 'hidden_dim': hparams['gating_hidden_dim'], 'cnn_features': hparams['gating_cnn_features'], 'cnn_kernel_size': hparams['gating_cnn_kernel_size'], 'pool_window': hparams['gating_pool_window']}\n\
    \          self.model = MoLR_v1(sota_params=sota_params, gating_params=gating_params, num_gamma_archetypes=hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n```"
thinking_content: '**Executing the "Mixture of Learned Ramps" (MoLR) v1 Experiment**


  Okay, the plan''s locked in. I''m ready to move from the Reflection Chamber to action. The goal is clear: implement and test the MoLR v1 architecture, which is essentially MoG v2 but with learned, non-linear ramps generated by an MLP. This will allow for potentially more complex and nuanced ramp profiles compared to the linear ones in MoG v2.


  The core hypothesis is that this modification will lead to improved performance. To make it a fair test, I''m sticking to the script. The experiment is designed as a direct comparison – keeping all hyperparameters and, critically, the `BASE_SEED` from the successful MoG v2 replication (ID 471) unchanged. This will ensure that any performance difference can be attributed to the new ramp design.


  My first step is to craft the new Flax module, `RampMLP`. It''s a straightforward MLP (`1 -> 8 -> 1 -> Sigmoid`), with a learnable scale parameter to control the dynamic range of the ramp. I''ll then integrate this `RampMLP` into the `MoLR_v1` model, which I''ll be modifying from the `MoG_v2` code.  The main changes are in the `__call__` method, where I''ll replace the linear ramp generation with the new `RampMLP` based generation and mixing process. I''ll create `E` independent `RampMLP` modules, generate the time step tensor, apply each MLP, and then mix the resulting ramps using the gating weights.


  The rest of the submission should be relatively easy. The submission script (`_define_hyperparameters`, `ModelWrapper`, etc.) will closely mirror the MoG v2 submission, the only change being that the `MoLR_v1` class is used. I just need to make sure the hyperparameters are copied correctly.


  Finally, I''ll submit this new code as an experiment and wait for the results. It''s a good plan, well controlled, and should give us a clear answer about whether the learned ramps are an improvement. Time to head to the Research Counter!

  '
