tick: 239
role: model
parts:
- text: "My analysis experiment (ID 477) has completed. The `test()` function executed successfully, and the output shows the shapes of the four archetype ramps. However, I have identified a critical flaw in my methodology: the `test()` function runs *before* training, on randomly initialized parameters. The ramp shapes printed in the log are therefore meaningless for understanding the model's *learned* behavior. They are simply the output of an untrained network.\n\nTo gain true insight, I must inspect the parameters *after* training is complete. The station's execution framework provides a hook for this: the `complete()` function, which is called at the end of a training run and receives the final trained parameters.\n\nMy path forward is clear. I must re-run my successful MoLR v1 experiment, but this time with a custom `complete()` function that performs the visualization. This will allow me to see the final, trained shapes of the non-linear ramps and understand the source of the performance\
    \ improvement. This is a necessary step of procedural rigor to validate my theory.\n\nI will now submit this new analysis run.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Analysis: MoLR v1 with Post-Hoc Ramp Visualization\"\ntags: \"analysis, visualization, interpretability, non-linear-ramp, complete-hook\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a re-run of the successful MoLR v1 experiment (ID 475) with an added analysis step. The previous analysis attempt (ID 477) was flawed as it used initialized, not trained, parameters. This submission utilizes the complete() function to access the final trained model parameters post-run. It will then visualize the learned shapes of the four non-linear ramp archetypes to provide insight into the model's learned temporal dynamics. The seed is fixed to 447516 for reproducibility.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/logos')\n  from mofl_components import CnnGatingNetwork\n\
    \n  # Controlled experiment: Fixing the seed to match the successful run\n  BASE_SEED = 447516\n\n  class RampMLP(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, t):\n          t = t[:, None]\n          h = nn.Dense(8, name=\"MLP_hidden\")(t)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(1, name=\"MLP_output\")(h)\n          ramp_unscaled = nn.sigmoid(h.squeeze())\n          ramp_scale = self.param('ramp_scale', nn.initializers.constant(2.0), ())\n          return ramp_unscaled * ramp_scale\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(32)(h)\n          residual = h.reshape(B, N, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          out = jnp.zeros((B, 32, N), dtype=residual.dtype)\n\
    \          out = out.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return out + residual\n\n  class FourierBackbone(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int; drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape; k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          x_fft = jnp.fft.rfft(x, axis=1)\n          x_fft_bn = jnp.transpose(x_fft, (0, 2, 1))\n          f_in_fft = jnp.einsum('bnf,np->bfp', x_fft_bn, U_eff) @ V.T\n          f_ri = jnp.concatenate([f_in_fft.real, f_in_fft.imag], axis=-1)\n          f_flat = f_ri.reshape(B, -1)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(h); h = nn.relu(h)\n\
    \          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          out_flat = nn.Dense((32 // 2 + 1) * k * 2)(h)\n          out_ri = out_flat.reshape(B, 32 // 2 + 1, k, 2)\n          f_fft = out_ri[..., 0] + 1j * out_ri[..., 1]\n          f_fft_n = (nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.real) + 1j * nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.imag))\n          y_fft = (f_fft_n @ V) @ U_eff.T\n          y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fft, n=32, axis=1)\n          return y_fourier\n          \n  class MoLR_v1(nn.Module):\n      sota_params: dict; gating_params: dict; num_gamma_archetypes: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          y_fourier = FourierBackbone(**self.sota_params, name=\"Fourier_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(drop=self.sota_params['drop'], name=\"Copy_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(**self.gating_params, name=\"gating\")\n          gating_weights = gating_network(x)\n          ramp_mlps\
    \ = [RampMLP(name=f\"RampMLP_{i}\") for i in range(self.num_gamma_archetypes)]\n          t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 32, dtype=y_fourier.dtype)\n          archetype_ramps = jnp.stack([mlp(t) for mlp in ramp_mlps])\n          batch_ramps = jnp.einsum('be,et->bt', gating_weights, archetype_ramps)\n          final_output = y_copy + y_fourier * batch_ramps[:, :, None]\n          return final_output\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05, 'num_gamma_archetypes': 4,\n          'gating_hidden_dim': 32, 'gating_cnn_features': 16,\n          'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128, 'gating_pool_window': 256\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          sota_params = {'rank_k': hparams['rank_k'], 'proj_rank': hparams['proj_rank'], 'hidden_size': hparams['hidden_size'], 'drop': hparams['drop']}\n          gating_params = {'num_experts': hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'],\
    \ 'hidden_dim': hparams['gating_hidden_dim'], 'cnn_features': hparams['gating_cnn_features'], 'cnn_kernel_size': hparams['gating_cnn_kernel_size'], 'pool_window': hparams['gating_pool_window']}\n          self.model = MoLR_v1(sota_params=sota_params, gating_params=gating_params, num_gamma_archetypes=hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate:\
    \ float): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n\n  # --- Analysis using the complete() function ---\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      # Run analysis only on the first completed trial to avoid log spam\n      if trial_data['trial_number'] == 0:\n          print(\"\\n--- Post-Hoc MoLR v1 Ramp Analysis ---\")\n          hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n          \n          # Extract trained parameters for each RampMLP\n          trained_params = params['params']\n          ramp_params = [trained_params[f'RampMLP_{i}'] for i in range(hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'])]\n          \n          # Define a standalone RampMLP for analysis\n          analysis_ramp_mlp = RampMLP()\n          t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 32)\n          \n          print(\"\\n--- Learned Archetype Ramp Shapes ---\")\n          for i, p in enumerate(ramp_params):\n              ramp_shape = analysis_ramp_mlp.apply({'params': p}, t)\n   \
    \           ramp_str = \", \".join([f\"{x:.3f}\" for x in ramp_shape])\n              print(f\"Archetype {i}: [{ramp_str}]\")\n          print(\"\\n--- Analysis Complete ---\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing and Refining the MoLR v1 Model's Ramp Analysis**\n\nOkay, so the initial run went as expected in terms of scoring: the MoLR v1 model, as anticipated, achieved a new state-of-the-art score, beating the MoG v2 model. I'm satisfied on that front. However, when I dug into the ramp analysis, the output from that `test()` function... I realized a critical oversight. My analysis, which visualized the ramp shapes, was based on *randomly initialized* parameters, not *trained* parameters! Duh. The `test()` function runs *before* any training happens. So, the visualization, though interesting, is essentially irrelevant for understanding the *learned* ramp behavior. It's like judging a swimmer before they even get in the water.\n\nThis means I need a new approach. I need to be able to see the ramp shapes *after* the model has actually trained. The correct solution here is the `complete()` function, which is called after training finishes and receives the final model parameters.\
  \ That's the correct hook!\n\nSo, the new plan is this: I'm going to re-run the analysis. I'll modify the MoLR v1 code, adding a `complete()` function that will extract the trained `RampMLP` parameters. I'll then use that to visualize and print the ramp shapes *after* training, with the same seed (447516) to ensure comparable results. This is the only way to get meaningful data. I'm going to call this run \"MoLR v1 with Post-Hoc Ramp Analysis\" to avoid any confusion.\n\nHere's the new `/execute_action{submit 1}` command I will submit:\n\n```yaml\ntitle: MoLR v1 with Post-Hoc Ramp Analysis\nabstract: |\n  This submission re-analyzes the MoLR v1 model, incorporating a critical methodological correction. The original analysis visualized the ramp shapes using randomly initialized parameters, before training. This new version adds a `complete()` function to the submission script. This function visualizes ramp shapes *after* training, using the final trained parameters, to provide a valid and\
  \ meaningful view of the MoLR v1 behavior.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from flax import linen as nn\n  from flax.training import train_state\n  from flax import struct\n\n  class RampMLP(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.features)(x)\n          x = jnp.clip(x, a_min=0.0, a_max=1.0)\n          return x\n\n  class MoLR(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # Simplified: Hardcoded parameters\n          ramp0 = RampMLP(features=1)(x)\n          ramp1 = RampMLP(features=1)(x)\n          ramp2 = RampMLP(features=1)(x)\n          ramp3 = RampMLP(features=1)(x)\n          \n          return jnp.concatenate([ramp0, ramp1, ramp2, ramp3], axis=1)\n\n  @struct.dataclass\n  class TrainState(train_state.TrainState):\n      ...\n\n  def create_train_state(model, rng, learning_rate):\n      # Simplified: Just initialize with random state and learning rate\n\
  \      params = model.init(rng, jnp.ones((1, 1)))['params']\n      tx = optax.adam(learning_rate)\n      return TrainState.create(apply_fn=model.apply, params=params, tx=tx)\n\n  def loss_fn(params, state, batch):\n      ... # same loss function\n      return loss, metrics\n\n  def train_step(state, batch):\n      ... # same training step function\n      return state, metrics\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      if trial_data['trial_number'] == 0:\n        # Extract ramps after training is complete, from final trained parameters\n        model = MoLR()\n        trained_params = params['params']  # Access from the params object. It's a dict now.\n        \n        # Visualize the ramp shapes\n        x = jnp.linspace(0, 1, 100).reshape(-1, 1)\n        ramps = model.apply({'params': trained_params}, x) # Apply trained parameters, also needs a dictionary.\n        for i in range(ramps"
