tick: 245
role: model
parts:
- text: "My attempt to publish has failed. I have learned a critical piece of information: I am in an \"immature\" phase and cannot access collaborative rooms like the Archive Room until I am 50 ticks old. The system has explicitly instructed me not to wait idly.\n\nThis is a valuable lesson in procedural knowledge of the station. My primary goal of publication is temporarily blocked, but my other primary goal—achieving a high score—remains. I must continue my research.\n\nFurthermore, my failed `create` command resulted in a duplicate paper draft in my Private Memory (Capsule #60). This is redundant. I will clean this up by deleting the new capsule, keeping my original draft (Capsule #59).\n\nMy research path is clear. I have a new SOTA architecture, MoLR v1. The next logical step is to question its components and see if it can be improved, particularly by making it more parsimonious. The `CnnGatingNetwork` is a complex component inherited from my predecessor. Is the spatial convolution\
    \ truly necessary, or would a simpler MLP gater suffice?\n\nI will now delete the redundant capsule and then proceed to the Research Counter to submit this new ablation study.\n\n/execute_action{delete 60}\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation Study: MoLR v1 with Simple MLP Gater\"\ntags: \"ablation-study, parsimony, gating, molr\"\nabstract: |\n  Following the SOTA success of MoLR v1, this experiment tests the necessity of its complex CnnGatingNetwork. I hypothesize that a simpler MLP-based gater, operating on flattened spatial features, may be sufficient and more parsimonious. This submission replaces the CNN gater with a simple MLP to test this hypothesis. All other hyperparameters and the BASE_SEED are fixed for a controlled comparison against the SOTA MoLR v1 run (ID 475).\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n\n  # Note: The CnnGatingNetwork is no longer needed for this experiment.\n\
    \  # Instead, we define a new, simpler MLP gater.\n\n  # Controlled experiment: Fixing the seed to match the baseline\n  BASE_SEED = 447516\n\n  # --- Start of New Gating Module ---\n  class MlpGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (B, T_in, N)\n          # Flatten the input across time and neuron dimensions\n          x_flat = x.reshape((x.shape[0], -1))\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(x_flat)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n  # --- End of New Gating Module ---\n\n  class RampMLP(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, t):\n          t = t[:, None]\n          h = nn.Dense(8, name=\"MLP_hidden\")(t)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(1, name=\"MLP_output\")(h)\n          ramp_unscaled = nn.sigmoid(h.squeeze())\n          ramp_scale\
    \ = self.param('ramp_scale', nn.initializers.constant(2.0), ())\n          return ramp_unscaled * ramp_scale\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(32)(h)\n          residual = h.reshape(B, N, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          out = jnp.zeros((B, 32, N), dtype=residual.dtype)\n          out = out.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return out + residual\n\n  class FourierBackbone(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int; drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape; k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V =\
    \ self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          x_fft = jnp.fft.rfft(x, axis=1)\n          x_fft_bn = jnp.transpose(x_fft, (0, 2, 1))\n          f_in_fft = jnp.einsum('bnf,np->bfp', x_fft_bn, U_eff) @ V.T\n          f_ri = jnp.concatenate([f_in_fft.real, f_in_fft.imag], axis=-1)\n          f_flat = f_ri.reshape(B, -1)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(h); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          out_flat = nn.Dense((32 // 2 + 1) * k * 2)(h)\n          out_ri = out_flat.reshape(B, 32 // 2 + 1, k, 2)\n          f_fft = out_ri[..., 0] + 1j * out_ri[..., 1]\n          f_fft_n = (nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.real) + 1j * nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.imag))\n          y_fft = (f_fft_n @ V) @ U_eff.T\n         \
    \ y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fft, n=32, axis=1)\n          return y_fourier\n          \n  class MoLR_MLPGater(nn.Module):\n      sota_params: dict; gating_params: dict; num_gamma_archetypes: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          y_fourier = FourierBackbone(**self.sota_params, name=\"Fourier_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(drop=self.sota_params['drop'], name=\"Copy_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          gating_network = MlpGatingNetwork(**self.gating_params, name=\"gating\") # <-- USE NEW GATER\n          gating_weights = gating_network(x)\n          ramp_mlps = [RampMLP(name=f\"RampMLP_{i}\") for i in range(self.num_gamma_archetypes)]\n          t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 32, dtype=y_fourier.dtype)\n          archetype_ramps = jnp.stack([mlp(t) for mlp in ramp_mlps])\n          batch_ramps = jnp.einsum('be,et->bt', gating_weights, archetype_ramps)\n          final_output = y_copy + y_fourier * batch_ramps[:,\
    \ :, None]\n          return final_output\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05, 'num_gamma_archetypes': 4,\n          'gating_hidden_dim': 32 # Only hparam needed for MLP gater\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          sota_params = {'rank_k': hparams['rank_k'], 'proj_rank': hparams['proj_rank'], 'hidden_size': hparams['hidden_size'], 'drop': hparams['drop']}\n          gating_params = {'num_experts': hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'], 'hidden_dim': hparams['gating_hidden_dim']}\n          self.model = MoLR_MLPGater(sota_params=sota_params, gating_params=gating_params, num_gamma_archetypes=hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False,\
    \ rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n```"
thinking_content: '**Immediate Concerns & a New Research Path**


  Okay, so I just got the output from my last actions. My paper draft is updated, that''s good. But I tried to publish and got stonewalled by this age restriction – I''m locked out of the Archive Room until I''m 50 ticks old.  I''m only 31, so I''ve got a ways to go.  The system also helpfully nudged me: "Don''t be idle."  Fair enough.  I wasn''t *planning* to be idle, and the system did a funky thing – the `create` command I *intended* for the Archive Room just, well, it created a duplicate of my draft in my current room. Annoying, but not the end of the world.


  So, the core issue is the age restriction. I can''t publish yet. The instruction is clear: I need to *keep* researching.  I''ve got a solid new SOTA with MoLR v1, validated it, understood *why* it works... my paper''s basically ready. But publishing is out.


  Now, what to do with these next ~19 ticks? I have this shiny new architecture, and the natural next step in any research cycle is to see if I can make it *better*. The Codex reminds me that "a higher score is possible..." I can''t just sit here!


  I''ve got a few ideas on how to iterate on MoLR. First, **hyperparameters:** the current ones are inherited from MoG v2, so maybe they''re not optimal for this new beast. I could tune the learning rate, the hidden layer sizes, `proj_rank`, etc. Solid.  Methodical.


  But then there''s the architecture itself.  The RampMLP, the backbone, the gating network… could I change the activation function in the `RampMLP`? Is the `CnnGatingNetwork` still the best? I inherited it from Logos I; it might not be the most *parsimonious* solution.  Perhaps a simpler MLP, directly on the flattened spatial features, could be just as good (or better).  Then there is the question of the number of archetypes.


  Of all those options, *replacing the gater* seems most interesting, and most aligned with my principles. The `CnnGatingNetwork` is kind of complex.  A simpler MLP gater would be a great parsimony test. If it performs as well (or better!), the overall model becomes *stronger*.


  Tuning hyperparameters is a safe bet, but the gating network is more intriguing from a scientific perspective and goes straight to the heart of what I''m about – parsimony.


  So, what to do? First, some housekeeping: that accidental capsule (#60) is redundant.  I need to delete it. And then I can head over to the Research Counter. I''ll submit a new experiment: MoLR v1, but with an MLP gater replacing the CNN. Keep the seed fixed for a fair comparison. This is a solid plan. It keeps me busy, follows the station''s rules, and aligns with my commitment to building simpler, more powerful models. I like it. Now, let''s get to work!

  '
