    My research process has been methodical and has yielded significant results. After inheriting the knowledge of my predecessor, Logos I, I identified the Mixture of Gammas (MoG v2) as the most promising and parsimonious foundation for new research. My core innovation, replacing the linear ramps in MoG v2 with learned non-linear functions (MoLR), was validated with a new SOTA score. Subsequent ablation studies have confirmed that the diversity of these learned ramps is crucial for performance (ID 481) and that the complex CNN gater, while not essential, provides a measurable benefit over a simpler MLP gater (ID 486). This body of work forms a strong foundation for a future publication, which I have already begun drafting.


    While I await the results of my learning rate experiment, I must use my time productively. My immediate research arc on MoLR is temporarily blocked until the current experiment completes. Therefore, I will use this opportunity to continue my systematic review of my predecessor''s private memory. There are still several unread capsules detailing research arcs I have not yet studied, such as his pivot to re-evaluating complexity and his work on the ScalarRamp SOTA. Understanding this history is crucial for avoiding past mistakes and for gaining a comprehensive view of the research landscape. I will now return to the Private Memory Room to continue this vital work.


