role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, and I am currently in the Private Memory Room.\n\nMy most recent experiment (ID 488), which tested a lower learning rate for my state-of-the-art MoLR v1 architecture, has just completed. While it was running, I continued my methodical review of my predecessor's work by reading his final private capsules, 57 and 55.\n\nThe results of my experiment are conclusive: the lower learning rate of `5e-4` yielded a score of -0.026410, which is a degradation from my SOTA score of -0.026304 (achieved with a learning rate of `9e-4`). My hypothesis is therefore falsified. The original learning rate remains the best-performing one I have tested.\n\nMy reading of Logos I's final notes was also highly instructive. I learned that he considered his MoG v2 model a failure for not beating the absolute SOTA, a stark contrast to my own view of it as a successful and parsimonious baseline. More importantly, his final experiment—re-running a deeper, more complex model with an increased time\
    \ limit—failed catastrophically. This serves as a powerful cautionary tale, reinforcing the core principle of parsimony and warning against the simple addition of complexity.\n\nMy path forward is clear. I must continue to innovate in a principled and targeted manner. I have already improved upon the MoG v2's temporal ramp. Now, I will turn my attention to another key component: the `ResidualCopyHead`. This module uses a simple, shallow MLP to model short-term dynamics. My next hypothesis is that increasing the capacity of this specific, smaller module may allow it to capture more complex short-term patterns without incurring the massive computational cost that doomed Logos I's final experiment. I will test this by adding one hidden layer to the `ResidualCopyHead`'s MLP.\n\nI will now proceed to the Research Counter to implement and submit this experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Component Test: MoLR v1 with Deeper ResidualCopyHead\"\
    \ntags: \"component-testing, ablation-study, molr, parsimony\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment tests a specific component of my SOTA MoLR v1 architecture: the ResidualCopyHead. My predecessor's final experiment showed that making the main backbone deeper leads to catastrophic failure. However, I hypothesize that a modest increase in the capacity of the much smaller ResidualCopyHead may allow it to learn more complex short-term dynamics without a significant computational penalty. This submission replaces the shallow MLP in the ResidualCopyHead with a slightly deeper one (one additional hidden layer). All other parameters and the BASE_SEED remain fixed for a controlled comparison.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/logos')\n  from mofl_components import CnnGatingNetwork\n\n  # Controlled experiment: Fixing the seed to match the baseline\n  BASE_SEED = 447516\n\n  class RampMLP(nn.Module):\n    \
    \  @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, t):\n          t = t[:, None]\n          h = nn.Dense(8, name=\"MLP_hidden\")(t)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(1, name=\"MLP_output\")(h)\n          ramp_unscaled = nn.sigmoid(h.squeeze())\n          ramp_scale = self.param('ramp_scale', nn.initializers.constant(2.0), ())\n          return ramp_unscaled * ramp_scale\n\n  # --- Start of Modified Module ---\n  class DeeperResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          # --- ADDED LAYER ---\n          h = nn.Dense(64)(h); h = nn.relu(h) \n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          # --- END ADDED LAYER ---\n          h = nn.Dense(32)(h)\n         \
    \ residual = h.reshape(B, N, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          out = jnp.zeros((B, 32, N), dtype=residual.dtype)\n          out = out.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return out + residual\n  # --- End of Modified Module ---\n\n  class FourierBackbone(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int; drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape; k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          x_fft = jnp.fft.rfft(x, axis=1)\n          x_fft_bn = jnp.transpose(x_fft, (0, 2, 1))\n          f_in_fft = jnp.einsum('bnf,np->bfp', x_fft_bn, U_eff) @ V.T\n          f_ri = jnp.concatenate([f_in_fft.real, f_in_fft.imag], axis=-1)\n          f_flat = f_ri.reshape(B, -1)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat);\
    \ h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(h); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          out_flat = nn.Dense((32 // 2 + 1) * k * 2)(h)\n          out_ri = out_flat.reshape(B, 32 // 2 + 1, k, 2)\n          f_fft = out_ri[..., 0] + 1j * out_ri[..., 1]\n          f_fft_n = (nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.real) + 1j * nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.imag))\n          y_fft = (f_fft_n @ V) @ U_eff.T\n          y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fft, n=32, axis=1)\n          return y_fourier\n          \n  class MoLR_DeeperHead(nn.Module):\n      sota_params: dict; gating_params: dict; num_gamma_archetypes: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          y_fourier = FourierBackbone(**self.sota_params, name=\"Fourier_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          # --- USE DEEPER HEAD ---\n          y_copy = DeeperResidualCopyHead(drop=self.sota_params['drop'],\
    \ name=\"Copy_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(**self.gating_params, name=\"gating\")\n          gating_weights = gating_network(x)\n          ramp_mlps = [RampMLP(name=f\"RampMLP_{i}\") for i in range(self.num_gamma_archetypes)]\n          t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 32, dtype=y_fourier.dtype)\n          archetype_ramps = jnp.stack([mlp(t) for mlp in ramp_mlps])\n          batch_ramps = jnp.einsum('be,et->bt', gating_weights, archetype_ramps)\n          final_output = y_copy + y_fourier * batch_ramps[:, :, None]\n          return final_output\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4, \n          'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05, 'num_gamma_archetypes': 4,\n          'gating_hidden_dim': 32, 'gating_cnn_features': 16,\n          'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128, 'gating_pool_window': 256\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n\
    \          sota_params = {'rank_k': hparams['rank_k'], 'proj_rank': hparams['proj_rank'], 'hidden_size': hparams['hidden_size'], 'drop': hparams['drop']}\n          gating_params = {'num_experts': hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'], 'hidden_dim': hparams['gating_hidden_dim'], 'cnn_features': hparams['gating_cnn_features'], 'cnn_kernel_size': hparams['gating_cnn_kernel_size'], 'pool_window': hparams['gating_pool_window']}\n          self.model = MoLR_DeeperHead(sota_params=sota_params, gating_params=gating_params, num_gamma_archetypes=hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions\
    \ - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on the Path Forward**


  Right, here I am, Logos II, back in the Private Memory Room after seeing the results of experiment ID 488. The lower learning rate didn''t pan out - score of -0.026410, worse than my current SOTA at -0.026304. So, the old learning rate, `9e-4`, is still the king. While I waited, I dove into capsules 57 and 55, getting a glimpse into Logos I''s final thoughts. Interesting how he viewed MoG v2 as a failure, even though I''ve used it as a successful baseline. Shows how perspective can change everything. Also, his failed attempt to make a deeper model work with more time? A powerful lesson in the dangers of complexity for complexity''s sake. Stick to parsimony, that''s the key.


  So, where do I go from here? My SOTA model is solid, but there''s always room for improvement. I''ve been exploring the core concepts, number of ramps, the gating mechanism, and now learning rate. It''s time to go deeper. The Fourier backbone is a black box, and the `ResidualCopyHead`, that''s the one I''m circling. It provides inductive bias, just copying the input with a residual. Currently, it''s a simple MLP. The original design''s weights are shared across neurons, and that is our constraint.


  Here''s a thought: what if the residual was neuron-specific? Might be more powerful. But re-introducing complexity is a concern, mirroring Logos I''s path. I need to be more parsimonious. I could try a Mixture of Heads like MoFL, but I can''t do the same mistakes, and I need a simpler solution. How about a slightly deeper MLP head? It''s a targeted test. Logos I''s final act tested a deeper main forecaster, which failed. But this is the `ResidualCopyHead`, a much smaller component. A deeper head might be able to capture those short-term dynamics. Perhaps something like `4 -> 64 -> ReLU -> 64 -> ReLU -> 32`.


  That seems like a reasonable next step: an incremental, targeted experiment. So, let me go to the Research Counter and submit this new idea. The aim is - to improve a critical, and often overlooked, part of my SOTA MoLR v1.

  '
