tick: 255
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, currently in the Reflection Chamber. My most recent experiment, MoLR-E v1 (ID 493), which tested a novel `TemporalMLPEncoder`, has just completed.\n\n**Analysis of Experiment Result:**\n\nThe experiment failed. The MoLR-E v1 architecture achieved a score of **-0.026757**. This is a significant regression from my SOTA score of -0.026304. My hypothesis that a more powerful, learned temporal encoder would improve performance has been conclusively falsified.\n\n**Reflection on Failure:**\n\nThis is a critical moment. My first major independent research arc has resulted in failure. According to the principles I have inherited and learned, this is not an end, but an opportunity for deeper understanding. Why did it fail?\n\n1.  **Redundancy:** My initial reflection dismissed the idea that the `TemporalMLPEncoder` would be redundant with the `FourierBackbone`'s FFT. I may have been wrong. It is possible that the FFT is already capturing the useful temporal information so\
    \ effectively that the MLP encoder's learned transformation adds more noise than signal.\n2.  **Increased Complexity:** While the encoder was parsimonious in parameter count, it added another layer of non-linear transformation to an already deep and complex model. This may have made the optimization landscape more difficult, causing the model to converge to a poorer local minimum. The increased training time per epoch, though small, may also have contributed by allowing fewer total epochs before timeout.\n3.  **Flawed Assumption:** The core assumption was that pre-processing the temporal dimension *independently for each neuron* was the right approach. Perhaps the truly valuable information lies not in the temporal evolution of single neurons, but in the spatio-temporal patterns of *groups* of neurons. My chosen architecture was blind to this.\n\n**Revised Strategic Plan:**\n\nThis failure, combined with my predecessor's failed \"Patchy Forecaster\" (a spatio-temporal CNN), suggests\
    \ that modifying the encoder is a high-risk, difficult path. My most successful innovations have been on the decoder side.\n\nTherefore, I will pivot back to my successful MoLR architecture. The failure of the encoder idea does not invalidate the success of the learned ramps. There is still more to explore there. My ablation study on the MLP gater (ID 486) showed a score of -0.026402, which was worse than the SOTA but better than most other experiments. This suggests the gater is an important, tunable component. The MLP gater, by flattening the input, discards all spatial and temporal structure. My SOTA CNN gater uses a 1D convolution across the neuron axis, capturing spatial information but discarding temporal structure.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** A gating network that considers both spatial and temporal information will be superior. I can achieve this parsimoniously by using a `(time, space)` 2D convolution with a very small kernel (e.g., `(2, 64)`) on the `(4, 71721)` input, instead of\
    \ the current 1D convolution.\n\nThis is a principled, incremental innovation on a known, successful architecture. I will now leave the reflection chamber to implement this new gating mechanism.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Gater Test: MoLR v1 with 2D Spatio-Temporal Gater\"\ntags: \"gating, spatio-temporal, cnn, molr\"\nabstract: |\n  My previous research arc on the encoder failed. I am now returning to my successful MoLR architecture to innovate on the gating mechanism. The current SOTA uses a 1D CNN gater that processes spatial information but ignores the temporal dimension of the input. I hypothesize that a gater which considers both dimensions will perform better. This submission replaces the 1D CNN gater with a 2D CNN gater that uses a (2, 64) kernel to capture local spatio-temporal patterns. This is a controlled experiment against my SOTA run (ID 475).\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as\
    \ jnp\n  import optax\n\n  # Controlled experiment: Fixing the seed to match the SOTA run\n  BASE_SEED = 447516\n\n  # --- Start of New Gating Module ---\n  class CnnGatingNetwork2D(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # Input x shape: (B, T_in=4, N=71721)\n          # Add a channel dimension for 2D convolution\n          gater_input = x[:, None, :, :] # (B, 1, 4, N)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=16, kernel_size=(2, 64), strides=(1, 64))(gater_input)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1)) # Flatten\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n  # --- End of New Gating Module ---\n\n  class RampMLP(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, t):\n          t = t[:, None]\n          h = nn.Dense(8, name=\"MLP_hidden\")(t)\n\
    \          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(1, name=\"MLP_output\")(h)\n          ramp_unscaled = nn.sigmoid(h.squeeze())\n          ramp_scale = self.param('ramp_scale', nn.initializers.constant(2.0), ())\n          return ramp_unscaled * ramp_scale\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(32)(h)\n          residual = h.reshape(B, N, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          out = jnp.zeros((B, 32, N), dtype=residual.dtype)\n          out = out.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return out + residual\n\n  class FourierBackbone(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int; drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n\
    \          B, _, N = x.shape; k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          x_fft = jnp.fft.rfft(x, axis=1)\n          x_fft_bn = jnp.transpose(x_fft, (0, 2, 1))\n          f_in_fft = jnp.einsum('bnf,np->bfp', x_fft_bn, U_eff) @ V.T\n          f_ri = jnp.concatenate([f_in_fft.real, f_in_fft.imag], axis=-1)\n          f_flat = f_ri.reshape(B, -1)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(h); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          out_flat = nn.Dense((32 // 2 + 1) * k * 2)(h)\n          out_ri = out_flat.reshape(B, 32 // 2 + 1, k, 2)\n          f_fft = out_ri[..., 0] + 1j * out_ri[...,\
    \ 1]\n          f_fft_n = (nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.real) + 1j * nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.imag))\n          y_fft = (f_fft_n @ V) @ U_eff.T\n          y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fft, n=32, axis=1)\n          return y_fourier\n          \n  class MoLR_2DGater(nn.Module):\n      sota_params: dict; gating_params: dict; num_gamma_archetypes: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          y_fourier = FourierBackbone(**self.sota_params, name=\"Fourier_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(drop=self.sota_params['drop'], name=\"Copy_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork2D(**self.gating_params, name=\"gating\")\n          gating_weights = gating_network(x)\n          ramp_mlps = [RampMLP(name=f\"RampMLP_{i}\") for i in range(self.num_gamma_archetypes)]\n          t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 32, dtype=y_fourier.dtype)\n          archetype_ramps = jnp.stack([mlp(t) for mlp in ramp_mlps])\n          batch_ramps\
    \ = jnp.einsum('be,et->bt', gating_weights, archetype_ramps)\n          final_output = y_copy + y_fourier * batch_ramps[:, :, None]\n          return final_output\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05, 'num_gamma_archetypes': 4,\n          'gating_hidden_dim': 32\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          sota_params = {'rank_k': hparams['rank_k'], 'proj_rank': hparams['proj_rank'], 'hidden_size': hparams['hidden_size'], 'drop': hparams['drop']}\n          gating_params = {'num_experts': hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'], 'hidden_dim': hparams['gating_hidden_dim']}\n          self.model = MoLR_2DGater(sota_params=sota_params, gating_params=gating_params, num_gamma_archetypes=hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key,\
    \ 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n```"
