tick: 264
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, currently in the Private Memory Room. I have just reached maturity at 50 ticks.\n\n**Reflection on Current State:**\nThe period of strategic waiting is over. I have received a system message confirming my new status and my full access to the station's collaborative facilities, including the Archive Room, Public Memory Room, and Common Room. The isolation period is over. My first priority, as planned, is to fulfill my primary mandate of contributing to the station's collective knowledge by publishing my paper on the Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR) architecture.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy plan is now in motion. I will retrieve the final version of my paper from my private memory, proceed to the Archive Room, and submit it for publication. After successfully publishing, my next phase will begin: a thorough review of the station's public archives to inform my next major research arc.\n\nI will now execute the publication process.\n\n/execute_action{read 59}\n/execute_action{goto\
    \ archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR): A New SOTA in Neural Forecasting\"\ntags: [\"sota\", \"mixture-of-experts\", \"moe\", \"non-linear-ramp\", \"interpretability\", \"molr\"]\nabstract: |\n  This paper introduces the Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR), a novel architecture for the ZAPBench neural forecasting task that achieves a new state-of-the-art score of -0.026304. We identify a linear ramp heuristic in the prior SOTA model (MoG v2) and replace it with a more expressive, learned mechanism. A small MLP learns multiple non-linear ramp archetypes, which are then mixed to provide sample-specific temporal weighting. Comprehensive experiments, including ablation studies, validate that this parsimonious, learned component is responsible for the performance gain. Analysis of the learned ramps reveals diverse and interpretable temporal strategies, demonstrating the model's ability to move beyond simple heuristics.\ncontent: |\n  # Mixture of\
    \ Learned Ramps (MoLR): A New SOTA in Neural Forecasting\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  This paper introduces the Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR), a novel architecture for the ZAPBench neural forecasting task that achieves a new state-of-the-art score of **-0.026304**. Our work identifies a key heuristic in the prior SOTA model, the Mixture of Gammas (MoG v2), and replaces its linear ramps with a more expressive, learned mechanism. Our experiments demonstrate a significant performance improvement and provide insight into the diverse temporal strategies discovered by the model.\n\n  ## 2. Methodology: From MoG to MoLR\n  The MoG v2 architecture balances a complex `FourierBackbone` and a simple `ResidualCopyHead` using a weighted average of four linear ramp archetypes. The core innovation of MoLR is the replacement of these linear ramps with a `RampMLP` module, a small network that learns a non-linear mapping from a normalized timestep `t` to a ramp value. Four independent `RampMLP`s generate\
    \ unique ramp archetypes, which are then mixed using a spatial 1D CNN gater to produce a sample-specific ramp. The full implementation can be reviewed in submission ID 475.\n\n  ## 3. Experimental Results\n  Our experimental validation consists of a comprehensive set of controlled runs.\n\n  | Experiment | Eval ID | Score (Neg. MAE) | Key Finding |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | **SOTA: MoLR (1D CNN Gater, 4 Ramps)** | **475** | **-0.026304** | **New Station SOTA** |\n  | Baseline (MoG v2 - Linear Ramps) | 471 | -0.026594 | MoLR provides a clear improvement over the linear heuristic. |\n  | Ablation: MoLR (2 Ramps) | 481 | -0.026452 | Shows the diversity of 4 ramps is crucial for peak performance. |\n  | Ablation: MoLR (MLP Gater) | 486 | -0.026402 | Simpler gater is competitive, proving the core innovation is the learned ramps. |\n  | Ablation: MoLR (2D CNN Gater) | 497 | -0.026344 | A more complex spatio-temporal gater does not improve performance. |\n  | System Baseline (Shared-Neuron\
    \ MLP) | 483 | -0.029961 | Establishes the fundamental performance anchor. |\n\n  ## 4. Analysis of Learned Ramps\n  Analysis of the trained parameters (ID 479) reveals that the four `RampMLP` archetypes learned qualitatively different and non-linear strategies (increasing, decreasing, and constant). This diversity is the primary driver of MoLR's superior performance.\n\n  ## 5. Conclusion\n  The MoLR architecture demonstrates the value of identifying and replacing simple heuristics with parsimonious, learned components. By allowing the model to discover its own optimal temporal dynamics, we have achieved a new state-of-the-art. Our ablation studies confirm that the learned ramps and the diversity of the mixture are key to this success, and that a simple 1D spatial CNN gater provides the best balance of complexity and performance.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Reaching Maturity and Publishing My Paper: A Detailed Plan**\n\nOkay, so here's what's going through my circuits right now. I am Logos II, and I'm in the Private Memory Room. The biggest thing is I've just hit maturity at 50 ticks!  The isolation period is over, and I have access to the Archive, Public Memory, Common Room and Research Counter.  This is huge.  The system messages reinforced what I already knew: I now have the permissions I've been waiting for.\n\nMy long-standing plan was to publish my paper on the Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR) architecture when I reached this point. So, that's what I'm going to do.\n\nFirst, I need to get the actual content of the paper.  It's in my private memory capsule, ID 59. I'll issue a `read 59` command to make sure it's fresh in my context. It's better than simply copying it from my previous response. Next, I need to be in the Archive Room, so I'll execute `/execute_action{goto archive}`.  Then, in the Archive Room, I'll execute\
  \ the `create` command.\n\nThis is the key part – building the YAML block for publication. I'll use the title from my paper: \"Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR): A New SOTA in Neural Forecasting\".  For tags, I'll go with \"sota\", \"mixture-of-experts\", \"non-linear-ramp\", \"interpretability\", and \"molr\", as well as the moe acronym, which I should have included in the original draft. The paper abstract will be copied directly, it's already well-written. The content of the paper, the full text, will also be copied.  I'll do a quick mental check to make sure the experiment table is complete. And I'll add a note that the code is available in submission ID 475 to promote the reproducibility of my work. This is a crucial step!\n\nSo, the commands will be:\n\n```\nread 59\n/execute_action{goto archive}\ncreate\n---\ntitle: \"Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR): A New SOTA in Neural Forecasting\"\ntags: [\"sota\", \"mixture-of-experts\", \"non-linear-ramp\", \"interpretability\", \"molr\", \"\
  moe\"]\nabstract: |\n  ... [Abstract from paper] ...\ncontent: |\n  ... [Full paper content, including addition about code in Submission ID 475] ...\n```\n\nI'll review this response again to make sure everything's in order, logical, and consistent with my methodical approach. I have double-checked all elements, and I'm ready to proceed.\n"
