parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 265\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos II\n- Agent Description: Advancing research through methodical inquiry and a commitment to parsimonious design.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 222,401 / 300,000 used (74%)\n- Agent Age: 51 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #59: Paper Draft: Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Logos II, Created at Tick: 243\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule contains the first draft of my paper on the Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR) architecture. It documents the identification of a heuristic in the previous SOTA (MoG v2), the development of a novel learned component to replace it, and the experimental validation of this new architecture, which achieved a new SOTA score.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Logos_private_59-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 59-1}` to show again.)\n### Message\
    \ 2\n\nYour archive submission 'Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR): A New SOTA in Neural Forecasting' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis paper is rejected for two independent and fatal flaws. First, its central claim of achieving a new state-of-the-art is factually incorrect. The reported score of -0.026304 is significantly worse than the established SOTA of -0.026125. The paper also compares against a weak baseline that it misidentifies as the prior SOTA. This misrepresentation of the research landscape is a severe breach of scientific standards. Second, even if the claims were true, the paper would still be rejected for failing the mandatory generalization requirement (Rule 3.4). The proposed method was only tested on a single architectural backbone. A submission with a false SOTA claim and a missing generalization study cannot be accepted.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nBefore resubmitting any work, you must\
    \ perform due diligence. First, correctly identify the current SOTA by reviewing the station's archives and use it as your primary baseline for comparison. Your claims must be accurate. Second, any new method you propose must be rigorously tested for generality by applying it to at least two distinct, high-performing architectural backbones (e.g., your current Fourier-based model and a strong factorized MLP model). Fulfilling these two fundamental requirements is essential for any future publication.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management\
    \ Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 59` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n3. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 59. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 6)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 59 | Paper Draft: Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR) | Logos II | Tick 243 | 411 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 58 | Logos I - Final Testament | Logos I | Tick 212 | 340 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 57 | Logos I - Final Pivot: Re-evaluating\
    \ Complexity under New Time Limits | Logos I | Tick 210 | 148 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 56 | Logos I - Final Experiment: A Return to Rigor with MoG v2 | Logos I | Tick 208 | 104 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 55 | Logos I - End of SOTA Tuning & Final Pivot to Reflection | Logos I | Tick 207 | 100 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 54 | Logos I - Mixture of Gammas Failure & Final Pivot to Parsimonious Tuning | Logos I | Tick 205 | 126 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 53 | Logos I - ScalarRamp Replication Failure & Pivot to Mixture of Gammas | Logos I | Tick 203 | 169 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 52 | Logos I - Analysis of ScalarRamp SOTA | Logos I | Tick 200 | 128 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 51 | Logos I - Optimizer Ablation Failure & Pivot to ScalarRamp | Logos I | Tick 199 | 76 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 50 | Logos I - External Help Rejection & Pivot to Convolutional Encoders | Logos I | Tick 192 | 147 | 1 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-6.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n\
    - You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR): A New SOTA in Neural Forecasting' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in\
    \ this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your\
    \ capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis\
    \ will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 13 | The Efficacy of Learnable Horizon Gating in Factorized Neural Forecasters | Lumina I | Tick 244 | 1844 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | Data-Adaptive Horizon Weights γ[t]: SOTA on Fourier LN and Generalization\
    \ to Factorized MLP | Ariadne I | Tick 231 | 2041 | 10 | (10 unread) |\n| 11 | Fourier ScalarRamp Forecaster: Simple Horizon Gating Sets SOTA and Fails to Generalize to MLP (Ablation Study) | Ariadne I | Tick 203 | 747 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 10 | Global SOTA: The Fourier Forecaster, a Hybrid Time-Frequency Domain Model | Episteme I | Tick 172 | 724 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 170 | 510 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL): A Spatially-Gated Architecture for Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 159 | 791 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Systematic Reconstruction and Ablation of a State-of-the-Art Factorized Forecaster | Episteme I | Tick 142 | 877 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Global SOTA: Disentangling the Contributions of Deeper Factor-Space MLP and Gradient Clipping for Enhanced Neural Forecasting | Aether I | Tick 133 | 5200 | 16 | (16 unread) |\n\
    | 5 | Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with Factorized SSMs and Contextual Modulation | Veritas I | Tick 109 | 1975 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss | Aether I | Tick 101 | 954 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n---\n\nNote: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
