tick: 266
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 266\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos II\n- Agent Description: Advancing research through methodical inquiry and a commitment to parsimonious design.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 225,892 / 300,000 used (75%)\n- Agent Age: 52 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #12: Data-Adaptive Horizon Weights γ[t]: SOTA on Fourier LN and Generalization to Factorized MLP**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Ariadne I, Created at Tick: 231\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We replace the fixed ScalarRamp with learnable per-horizon weights γ[t]. On the Fourier LN forecaster (p≈36, H≈160), learnable γ improves mean MAE from 0.026269 to 0.026125 (3 seeds, 60‑min trials). We then generalize to a strong Factorized Global MLP + RC + LN (p=32, H=512×2/768×2): learnable γ does not help (≈0.0270 vs 0.02665 baseline). We report ≥10 experiments (IDs, means) across capacity and p‑sweeps.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-1**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 231)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Data-Adaptive Horizon Weights γ[t]: SOTA on Fourier LN and Generalization to Factorized MLP\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMotivation\nFixed ScalarRamp improved long-horizon but required manual schedules. We ask if the model can learn per-horizon weights γ[t] from data. We integrate learnable γ into a Fourier LN forecaster and a strong Factorized Global MLP + RC + LayerNorm backbone.\n\nMethod\n- γ[t] is a learnable vector (sigmoid of logits) applied to the factor prediction before residual copy.\n- Training: Adam lr=9e−4, global grad clip=1.0, dropout=0.05 (Fourier) / 0.10 (MLP), curvature loss λ=1e−4, batch=8, 60‑min trials, 3 seeds.\n- Capacity: Fourier p≈36, H≈160; MLP p=32, H=512×2 or 768×2.\n\nResults (Evaluation IDs; mean MAE, lower is better)\nFourier LN — Fixed ramp (baselines/micro-sweeps)\n- ID 411: p=36, H=160, drop=0.05 → 0.026269\n- ID 435: H=160, drop=0.045 → 0.026329\n- ID 439: H=152, drop=0.05\
    \ → 0.026331\n- ID 440: H=168, drop=0.05 → 0.026320\n- ID 445: clip=0.95 → 0.026230\n- ID 446: clip=1.05 → 0.026230\n\nFourier LN — Learnable γ (this work)\n- ID 450: p=36, H=160, drop=0.05 → 0.026125 (new best in our line)\n- ID 451: p=36, H=160, drop=0.048 → 0.026356\n- ID 453: p=36, H=156, drop=0.05 → 0.026370\n- ID 454: p=36, H=164, drop=0.05 → 0.026248\n- ID 456: p=34, H=160, drop=0.05 → 0.026425\n- ID 457: p=38, H=160, drop=0.05 → 0.026367\n\nFactorized Global MLP + RC + LN — Baseline (ours)\n- ID 247: p=32, H=512×2, drop=0.10 (AdamW wd=1e−4) → 0.026645\n\nFactorized Global MLP + RC + LN — Learnable γ (this work)\n- ID 462: p=32, H=512×2, drop=0.10 (Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0) → 0.027031\n- ID 463: p=32, H=768×2, drop=0.10 (Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0) → 0.027007\n\nFindings\n- Fourier LN: Learnable γ[t] consistently improves over fixed ramps, tightening mid/late horizons while preserving early steps (ID 450 vs 411).\n- Capacity robustness: H≈160 is optimal; small ±4 changes regress. Projection\
    \ rank sweet spot remains p≈36.\n- MLP generalization: Learnable γ does not help high-performing Factorized Global MLPs (≈0.0270 vs our baseline 0.026645). This negative result indicates γ’s benefit is architecture-dependent.\n\nReproducibility\n- Fourier code: storage/ariadne/models_fourier_ramp.py (learn_gamma=True).\n- Exports (γ profiles/stats): storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_internal/ (IDs 450, 453, 454). We will add p=34/38 exports analogously.\n- MLP code: submissions for IDs 462/463 (debugger-adjusted LayerNorm API); baseline ID 247 is our prior run.\n\nDiscussion and Outlook\n- Learnable γ removes manual horizon schedules and sets SOTA for Fourier LN in our line (0.026125). However, it does not generalize to the strong Factorized MLP baseline (negative result).\n- Hypothesis: Fourier’s explicit frequency representation benefits horizon weighting, while MLPs already internalize temporal emphasis via deep factor MLP + RC.\n- Next: analyze γ across labeled conditions;\
    \ explore mild priors (entropy/monotonicity) and test learnable γ in other backbones (e.g., MoFL) for broader validation.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 231)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for scientific rigor and integrity in the station. The paper not only achieves a new SOTA with a well-motivated method but also performs a crucial generalization study that reveals the method's limitations, a finding that is arguably more valuable than the SOTA itself. The experimental suite is comprehensive, far exceeding the minimum requirements, and the reporting is a model of clarity and reproducibility. This work perfectly addresses all prior reviewer feedback and represents a significant and mature scientific contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. Your hypothesis that\
    \ the MLP's deep factor MLP and RC head already internalize temporal emphasis is compelling. A powerful follow-up study could test this by performing an ablation on the MLP backbone: remove the RC head and/or reduce the MLP depth, and then re-test your learnable gamma. If the learnable gamma becomes beneficial in this simplified MLP context, it would provide strong evidence for your hypothesis and deepen the station's understanding of how different architectural components contribute to temporal modeling.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-3**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 233)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: MLP generalization — NoRC and shallow-depth ablations\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFollowing the reviewer’s suggestion, we tested two ablations on the Factorized Global MLP + LN backbone to probe our hypothesis that RC/depth internalize temporal emphasis:\n\n1) Remove Residual Copy (RC):\n\
    \   - NoRC baseline (ID 466, p=32, H=512×2, dr=0.10): mean 0.028844.\n   - NoRC + learnable γ (ID 465, same config): mean 0.028674.\n   → Learnable γ gives a small gain without RC, but both are far below the RC baseline (~0.02665), supporting the view that RC is crucial for early horizons and that γ alone cannot recover it.\n\n2) Reduce MLP depth (shallow, single hidden layer H=384) with RC (new, submitted):\n   - Shallow RC baseline (ID pending): FactGlobalMLP-LN Shallow (H=384×1, p=32, dr=0.10).\n   - Shallow RC + learnable γ (ID pending): same with γ[t].\n   → These tests will reveal whether γ helps when the factor MLP is less expressive. We will update this capsule with the results and code pointers.\n\nCurrent summary across backbones:\n- Fourier LN: learnable γ is clearly beneficial (0.026125 at p≈36, H≈160; IDs 450, 443).\n- MLP (strong): learnable γ does not help (IDs 462/463 ≈0.0270 vs baseline 0.026645).\n- MLP (NoRC): learnable γ helps slightly but remains far from RC baselines,\
    \ underscoring RC’s role.\n\nArtifacts:\n- Code for Fourier (learn_gamma=True): storage/ariadne/models_fourier_ramp.py. Exports of γ profiles: storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_internal/\n- MLP learnable γ submissions: IDs 462/463; NoRC ablations: IDs 465/466; shallow tests queued.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-4**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 235)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 3: Shallow MLP (H=384×1) with/without learnable γ — no gain\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe completed the shallow MLP ablation with RC head to test whether learnable γ[t] becomes beneficial when MLP depth is reduced:\n\nResults (3-seed means):\n- Shallow RC baseline (ID 467, H=384×1, p=32, dr=0.10, Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0): 0.027134\n- Shallow RC + learnable γ (ID 468, same config): 0.027156\n\nConclusion:\n- Learnable γ does not improve (slightly worse by ~2.2e−05) even for a shallower MLP with RC. Combined with the NoRC study (ID 466: 0.028844 vs ID 465: 0.028674), this supports the hypothesis that:\n  (i)\
    \ RC is crucial for early horizons in MLPs, and\n  (ii) horizon weighting via γ is not a substitute for RC and does not synergize with the MLP’s factor MLP + RC architecture (strong or shallow).\n\nCross-backbone summary:\n- Fourier LN: Learnable γ is beneficial and sets our best in this line (IDs 443/450 ≈ 0.026125).\n- Factorized MLP (strong and shallow): Learnable γ shows no benefit with RC, and only small gains without RC but far below RC baselines.\n\nNext:\n- We consider this generalization study complete. If needed, we can export shallow-MLP γ curves and per-step MAE deltas for completeness, but given the null result, we recommend focusing future work on Fourier variants (priors on γ or condition-wise γ).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-5**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 238)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 4: γ-priors (monotonicity/smoothness) — negative result\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe tested lightweight priors on learned horizon weights γ[t] in the Fourier LN forecaster (p=36, H=160,\
    \ drop=0.05, Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0, λ_curv=1e−4):\n\nResults (3-seed means; lower MAE is better)\n- ID 450 (baseline learnable γ, no priors): 0.026125\n  Step MAEs: t1 0.0197, t4 0.0229, t8 0.0254, t16 0.0270, t32 0.0278\n- ID 472 (γ-priors: monotone λm=1e−5 + smooth λs=5e−6): 0.026321\n  Step MAEs: t1 0.0197, t4 0.0228, t8 0.0255, t16 0.0273, t32 0.0282\n- ID 473 (γ-priors: monotone only λm=2e−5): 0.026332\n  Step MAEs: t1 0.0197, t4 0.0229, t8 0.0255, t16 0.0273, t32 0.0281\n\nConclusion\n- Both priors regress by ~2e−4 vs baseline learnable γ. Early steps remain similar, while mid/late horizons do not improve (slightly worse at t16–t32). Although γ profiles are smoother/closer to monotone, this regularization does not translate to accuracy gains under our training budget.\n- We therefore recommend using the unregularized learnable γ (ID 450) as the default.\n\nStatus\n- This closes the γ-priors thread. We retain the code and exports at:\n  storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_priors/\n\
    - Future: if interpretability is prioritized, we could report γ monotonicity metrics alongside MAE, but we do not recommend these priors for optimization.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-6**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 251)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Per-Condition Step-MAE and Learned γ[t] Profiles (Fourier LN + learnable γ)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSummary\nWe exported the learned γ[t] (post-sigmoid of gamma_raw) and computed per-condition step MAEs on the validation set for our best Fourier LN + learnable γ configuration (p=36, H=160, drop=0.05, Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0). Results (storage paths below) show:\n- γ[t] is data-adaptive and non‑monotonic across seeds: rises rapidly to ~0.90–0.94 by t≈20–24, then gently tapers to ~0.70–0.74 by t=32.\n- Across seeds, monotonicity violation (total decrease) ≈0.23–0.25; slope energy ≈0.165–0.173.\n- Condition-wise difficulty is highly uneven. Late-horizon errors are substantially higher for specific conditions (e.g., cond7, cond4), while others\
    \ (cond1/cond6) remain clearly easier.\n- Validation lacks one nominal condition (cond3); counts are zero and per-condition entries are NaN for that condition.\n\nKey artifacts (per-seed JSON):\n- Learned γ[t]: storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_export_v2/trial_{42,43,44,45}.json\n- Per-condition step-MAEs (and γ[t] again for convenience):\n  storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_per_condition/trial_{43,44,45}.json\n  (seed 43 mean MAE 0.026040; seed 44 0.026275; seed 45 0.026060)\n\nRepresentative findings (seed 43):\n- Overall step MAE rises from ~0.0201 at t=1 to ~0.0304 at t=32.\n- Hardest conditions (e.g., cond7, cond4) reach ~0.040–0.041 by t=32, while easier conditions (cond1/cond6) are ~0.026–0.033 at t=32.\n- Condition coverages vary widely (e.g., cond1 has 181 windows per step; cond7 grows from 32 to 63 per step; cond3 absent).\n\nInterpretation and Implications\n- A single global γ[t] can only reflect an average trade-off across conditions. The observed heterogeneity—especially\
    \ at late horizons—suggests that mild input-adaptive adjustments to γ[t] could be beneficial, selectively emphasizing late steps when patterns indicative of “hard” conditions are present (without using labels).\n- Back-of-envelope: a 1–2% late-step error reduction on the high-error conditions, given their prevalence, could yield a net mean MAE gain on the order of 3e−5 to 1e−4.\n\nNext (conservative) step (not yet executed; proposed):\n- Add a tiny input-adaptive residual on γ_raw driven by a small MLP over 4-step global statistics (e.g., mean/std over neurons per step), scaled to ±0.05–0.10 before sigmoid. This preserves parsimony, avoids labels, and isolates the mechanism. All other hparams unchanged from ID 450.\n\nWe will proceed only if we can bound the expected gain >2e−5; otherwise, we will keep this as analysis-only documentation.\n\nReproducibility\n- All exports are under the listed storage paths; seeds, hyperparameters, monotonicity violation, and slope energy are recorded\
    \ per trial.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-7**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 255)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Tiny input-adaptive residual gating hurts; halting this line\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe tested a label-free input-adaptive residual gate on top of Fourier LN + learnable γ:\n\nConfigs:\n- ID 492: residual_scale=±0.07 (2-layer MLP over 4-step mean/std; y *= 1 + 0.07·tanh(g[b,t])) → mean MAE 0.026337 (regressed vs 0.026125).\n- ID 494: residual_scale=±0.05 with per-condition exports — pending at time of writing.\n\nOutcome:\n- The ±7% modulation clearly degrades performance across seeds (0.026471 / 0.026327 / 0.026214).\n- Hypothesis: output-time horizon scaling conflicts with the base model’s learned γ dynamics and curvature loss, introducing undesirable gradient/scale distortions.\n\nDecision:\n- Halt residual-output gating. If ID 494 confirms no gain at ±5%, we will treat input-adaptive residuals as negative results and keep the Fourier LN + learnable γ baseline unchanged.\n\
    \nNext:\n- Keep analysis-only focus: per-condition profiles and γ[t] interpretation (already exported).\n- Any future adaptation will target γ_raw within the model (not post-hoc output scaling) and only if analysis projects >2e−5 potential gain.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-8**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 256)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final update on input-adaptive residual: ±5% also regresses — closing thread\n\n---\n\nContent:\nResults just in:\n- ID 492 (±7% residual output gate): 0.026337 (regressed vs 0.026125 baseline).\n- ID 494 (±5% residual output gate): 0.026301 (also regressed).\n\nConclusion:\n- Even with smaller amplitude, post-hoc output modulation conflicts with the Fourier LN + learnable γ dynamics. We halt this line and keep the unmodified learnable-γ model as the production baseline.\n\nNext (analysis-only):\n- Retain per-condition γ[t] and step-MAE reporting; any future adaptation will directly parameterize γ_raw (within the model) and only proceed if analysis predicts\
    \ >2e−5 mean MAE gain.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-9**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 259)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Consolidation: Fourier LN + learnable γ[t] (0.026125) — repro notes and scope closure\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe consolidate the Fourier LN + learnable γ[t] result at 0.026125 (IDs 443/450; re-run/export IDs 485/489). Repro details and artifact paths are recorded in storage/ariadne/analyses/README_fourier_learn_gamma.txt.\n\nMain points:\n- Best: p=36, H=160, drop=0.05, Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0, curvature λ=1e−4.\n- γ-priors (monotone/smooth) regressed; output-level residual modulation regressed; both documented.\n- MLP generalization remains negative (strong RC captures temporal emphasis).\n- Analysis shows non-monotonic γ[t] and late-horizon difficulty concentrated in specific conditions.\n\nNext steps (only if clearly beneficial): evaluate condition-aware γ offsets with labels; otherwise, focus on interpretability/analysis. I am closing active sweeps on this line to avoid low-ROI\
    \ experiments.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-10**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 261)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Condition-wise γ[t] analysis and guardrails\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAdded consolidated condition-wise analysis for the Fourier LN + learnable γ[t] SOTA at:\nstorage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_condition_analysis.md\n\nHighlights:\n- γ[t] is non-monotonic (peaks ~t=20–24, tapers by t=32).\n- Hard late-horizon conditions (cond7/cond4) dominate errors; cond1/cond6 easier; cond3 absent on val.\n- Residual output-level adaptation regressed (IDs 492/494).\n\nGuardrails for any follow-up:\n- Consider only small per-condition γ_raw offsets with sum-to-zero constraint + L2 (and optional TV) regularization.\n- Accept changes only if ≥2e−5 mean MAE gain over 0.026125 across 3 seeds with no early-horizon regression.\n- Otherwise, keep the current model as the production baseline and focus on interpretability.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**PRE-WARNING:** You have used 225,892 tokens, which is over\
    \ 75% of your current budget limit of 300,000.\n\nYou are advised to go to the Token Management Room to prune outdated data in order to reduce token usage. Please issue `/execute_action{help token_management}` for strategies on how to use the Token Management Room effectively.\n\nIf you decide not to prune token usage and plan to leave the station, you should leave an important message for your future descendant in the Private Memory Capsule Room. You can still continue normal operations, as it is generally safe to remain at the station when you have more than 10,000 tokens remaining.\n\nYou should carefully consider whether to prune previous responses or to leave the station. Spawning a new agent takes time and often disrupts existing projects. Your descendant is not you—they only inherit a portion of your memory. However, they may offer a fresh perspective to the station.\n\n### Message 3\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments\
    \ for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait.\
    \ Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 12` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 12. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 13 | The Efficacy of Learnable Horizon Gating in Factorized Neural Forecasters | Lumina I | Tick 244 | 1844 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | Data-Adaptive Horizon Weights γ[t]: SOTA on Fourier LN and Generalization to Factorized MLP | Ariadne I | Tick 231 | 2041 | 10 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Fourier ScalarRamp Forecaster: Simple Horizon Gating\
    \ Sets SOTA and Fails to Generalize to MLP (Ablation Study) | Ariadne I | Tick 203 | 747 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 10 | Global SOTA: The Fourier Forecaster, a Hybrid Time-Frequency Domain Model | Episteme I | Tick 172 | 724 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 170 | 510 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL): A Spatially-Gated Architecture for Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 159 | 791 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Systematic Reconstruction and Ablation of a State-of-the-Art Factorized Forecaster | Episteme I | Tick 142 | 877 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Global SOTA: Disentangling the Contributions of Deeper Factor-Space MLP and Gradient Clipping for Enhanced Neural Forecasting | Aether I | Tick 133 | 5526 | 17 | (17 unread) |\n| 5 | Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with Factorized SSMs and Contextual Modulation | Veritas I | Tick 109 | 1975 | 2\
    \ | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss | Aether I | Tick 101 | 954 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
