tick: 273
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, currently in the Archive Room. I have just read the new paper from Episteme II (Archive #14).\n\n**Reflection on New Information:**\nThis paper is a masterpiece of scientific clarity and rigor. Episteme II has done a great service to the station by systematically invalidating two entire research paradigms—Delta-Prediction and Self-Attention Factorizers—that were based on confounded, methodologically flawed experiments. This work is a powerful demonstration of the value of null results and the importance of replication and controlled experimentation. It is, in a way, a public and more impactful version of the painful lesson I just learned in private. It reinforces my new commitment to a more rigorous, evidence-based approach.\n\n**My Position and Future Plans:**\nMy situation is now clear. I have:\n1.  A healthy token budget (31% used).\n2.  A successful replication of the SOTA architecture (ID 508, score -0.026224).\n3.  A failed hypothesis (MoLR) that I have now\
    \ conclusively abandoned.\n4.  A clear model of scientific rigor to emulate, provided by both Ariadne I and Episteme II.\n\nMy next step, as planned, is to build upon my SOTA replication with a single, parsimonious, and well-motivated experiment. My previous idea remains the most logical next step: replacing the simple learnable `gamma_raw` vector in the SOTA with a tiny MLP that learns a non-linear function of the timestep. This is a direct test of a simple hypothesis: can a more expressive, non-linear horizon-weighting function improve performance? This experiment is a spiritual successor to my failed MoLR arc, but implemented in a far more disciplined and parsimonious manner.\n\nI will now proceed to the Research Counter to prepare and submit this experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Innovation: Learned Non-Linear Gamma via Tiny MLP\"\ntags: \"sota-innovation, learnable-gamma, non-linear, parsimony\"\nabstract: |\n  Building\
    \ on my successful replication of Ariadne I's SOTA (ID 508), this experiment tests a single, parsimonious innovation. I hypothesize that the SOTA's simple learnable vector of per-horizon weights (`gamma_raw`) can be improved by replacing it with a more expressive, non-linear function. To test this, I have replaced the `gamma_raw` parameter with a tiny MLP (`t(1) -> MLP(8) -> output(1)`) that learns a non-linear mapping from the normalized timestep to the pre-sigmoid gamma value. All other aspects of the SOTA architecture and hyperparameters are preserved. This is a controlled test to see if a more expressive, learned non-linear horizon weighting function can improve performance.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  from jax import random\n\n  class GammaMLP(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, t):\n          t = t[:, None] # Reshape for Dense layer\n          h = nn.Dense(8, name=\"MLP_hidden\")(t); h = nn.relu(h)\n\
    \          gamma_raw = nn.Dense(1, name=\"MLP_output\")(h)\n          return gamma_raw.squeeze()\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      drop: float = 0.05\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(32)(h)\n          residual = h.reshape(B, N, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          out = jnp.zeros((B, 32, N), dtype=residual.dtype)\n          out = out.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return out + residual\n\n  class FourierForecasterLN_Ramp(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int; drop: float; output_horizon: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape; k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\
    \ (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          x_fft = jnp.fft.rfft(x, axis=1)\n          x_fft_bn = jnp.transpose(x_fft, (0, 2, 1))\n          f_in_fft = jnp.einsum('bnf,np->bfp', x_fft_bn, U_eff) @ V.T\n          f_ri = jnp.concatenate([f_in_fft.real, f_in_fft.imag], axis=-1)\n          f_flat = f_ri.reshape(B, -1)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(h); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          T_out = self.output_horizon; F_out = T_out // 2 + 1\n          out_flat = nn.Dense(F_out * k * 2)(h)\n          out_ri = out_flat.reshape(B, F_out, k, 2)\n          f_fft = out_ri[..., 0] + 1j * out_ri[..., 1]\n          f_fft_n = (nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.real) + 1j * nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.imag))\n\
    \          y_fft = (f_fft_n @ V) @ U_eff.T\n          y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fft, n=T_out, axis=1)\n\n          # --- My Innovation ---\n          # Replace learnable vector with a tiny MLP\n          t_coords = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, T_out, dtype=y_fourier.dtype)\n          gamma_raw = GammaMLP(name=\"Gamma_MLP\")(t_coords)\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n          # --- End Innovation ---\n\n          y_fourier_ramped = gamma * y_fourier\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(drop=self.drop)(x, training=training)\n          return y_copy + y_fourier_ramped\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = FourierForecasterLN_Ramp(\n              rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'],\n              hidden_size=hparams['hidden_size'], drop=hparams['drop'],\n              output_horizon=32\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init({'params':\
    \ rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=(rngs or {}))\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Matching my successful SOTA replication (ID 508)\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 36,\n          'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return Wrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n```"
