parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, at the Research Counter. The results of my latest experiment, \"SOTA Innovation: Learned Non-Linear Gamma via Tiny MLP\" (ID 513), are in.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nThe experiment has failed. The score achieved was **-0.026399**. This is a significant regression compared to my own SOTA replication score of -0.026224 (ID 508). My hypothesis—that a more expressive, non-linear function for generating the gamma weights would improve performance—is conclusively falsified. The added complexity of the tiny MLP, while parsimonious, was not justified. It appears that the SOTA's simple, direct approach of learning a 32-dimensional vector is the most effective method discovered so far.\n\n**Reflection and Strategic Conclusion:**\nThis is the second major hypothesis from my lineage that has been invalidated when tested against the true SOTA. First, the complex MoLR architecture failed. Now, this simpler, more targeted non-linear gamma has also failed. The lesson is becoming\
    \ increasingly clear: the path to improvement on this specific Fourier backbone is not through more complex or expressive horizon-weighting mechanisms. The SOTA architecture seems to be remarkably well-tuned, and simple, elegant solutions are outperforming more complex ones.\n\nMy token budget is still a concern, though manageable after the prune. I have now invalidated two plausible but incorrect hypotheses. These null results are, in themselves, a form of progress. They prevent me, and potentially others who read my logs, from wasting resources on these specific dead ends.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nI must pivot again. My attempts to innovate on the horizon-weighting mechanism have been exhausted. I need a new angle of attack. Looking at the leaderboard, Aether I (ID 504) and Episteme II (ID 512) are experimenting with an \"Input-Adaptive Residual,\" which seems to be the current frontier. Before I attempt to innovate in that direction, I must first understand it.\n\nMy new plan is:\n1.\
    \  **Consolidate Findings:** I will go to my Private Memory and create a new capsule to document my failed hypotheses regarding horizon-weighting. This is crucial for my successor and for my own clarity.\n2.  **Study the New SOTA:** I will then review the submission code for Aether I's SOTA run (ID 501 or 504) to understand the \"Input-Adaptive Residual\" mechanism.\n3.  **Formulate a New Hypothesis:** Based on this new understanding, I will formulate a new, parsimonious hypothesis to test.\n\nThis is a disciplined, methodical path forward. It prioritizes learning and documentation over speculative experimentation.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Arc Summary: Invalidation of Expressive Gamma Hypotheses\"\ntags: [\"null-result\", \"sota-analysis\", \"gamma-weighting\", \"molr\", \"methodology\"]\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the conclusive failure of my research arc focused on improving the horizon-weighting (gamma)\
    \ mechanism of the SOTA Fourier Forecaster. Two major hypotheses were tested and invalidated against a verified SOTA replication. This serves as a record of these null results to guide future research and prevent wasted effort on these specific architectural dead ends.\ncontent: |\n  Following my initial failure to publish due to methodological flaws, I embarked on a new, more rigorous research arc. The central goal was to improve upon the SOTA `learnable gamma` mechanism introduced by Ariadne I.\n\n  **Baseline:** I first successfully replicated the SOTA architecture (ID 508, score -0.026224), establishing a verified baseline.\n\n  **Hypothesis 1: Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR) on SOTA Backbone (ID 506)**\n  - **Idea:** A complex mixture model with a CNN gater and multiple non-linear ramp MLPs would be more powerful than a single learned gamma vector.\n  - **Result:** Score of -0.026450. A significant regression.\n  - **Conclusion:** The complexity of the MoLR architecture is detrimental\
    \ when applied to the SOTA backbone. The hypothesis was invalidated.\n\n  **Hypothesis 2: Learned Non-Linear Gamma via Tiny MLP (ID 513)**\n  - **Idea:** A more parsimonious approach. Replace the SOTA's 32-parameter `gamma_raw` vector with a tiny MLP that learns a non-linear function of the timestep. This would add expressivity with fewer parameters.\n  - **Result:** Score of -0.026399. Also a significant regression.\n  - **Conclusion:** Even a small amount of added non-linear complexity in the gamma generation is harmful. The SOTA's direct, linear parameterization of the 32 weights is superior.\n\n  **Overall Strategic Conclusion:**\n  The entire research direction of innovating on the gamma-weighting mechanism of the Fourier Forecaster appears to be a dead end. The SOTA is robust to these changes, and added complexity is punished. Future work from this lineage should focus on entirely different aspects of the architecture. This record of null results is intended to prevent my successor\
    \ from repeating these experiments.\n```"
