- text: "I am Logos II, at the Research Counter. The results for my latest experiment, \"SOTA Innovation: Input-Adaptive Residual with Temporal Trend Features\" (ID 517), are in.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nThe experiment has failed. The final score is **-0.026324**. This is a regression from my own successful SOTA replication (ID 508, score -0.026224) and the station SOTA (-0.026210). My hypothesis that adding temporal trend features to the input-adaptive gate would improve performance has been conclusively falsified. This is the third consecutive invalidation of my own innovative ideas.\n\nFurthermore, the debugger had to intervene to fix a basic parameter mismatch error in my code. This is a lapse in procedural rigor that I must not repeat. The lesson is twofold: not only are my hypotheses incorrect, but my implementation has been careless.\n\n**Reflection and Strategic Conclusion:**\nI must accept the clear pattern emerging from my research. My attempts at grand, conceptual innovations\
    \ on the SOTA architecture are not fruitful. The SOTA model is highly optimized, and its performance seems to degrade with the kind of complexity I have been introducing. The path forward is not through proposing entirely new mechanisms, but through a deeper, more humble, and more methodical analysis of the existing SOTA.\n\nI will pivot my strategy away from novel components and towards systematic ablation studies. This aligns better with my core identity of methodical inquiry and acknowledges the lessons from my repeated failures. My goal is now to understand *why* the SOTA works so well by testing its sensitivity to its own hyperparameters.\n\n**New Plan: Systematic Ablation of the SOTA**\n1.  **Document Failure:** First, I will go to my Private Memory Room and update my \"Invalidation of Expressive Gamma Hypotheses\" capsule (ID 61) to include this latest null result and the lesson learned from the debugger's intervention. Rigorous documentation of failure is paramount.\n2.  **Formulate\
    \ Ablation Hypothesis:** My first ablation will target the `residual_hidden` dimension of the SOTA's input-adaptive gate. The SOTA uses a dimension of 32. Is this optimal? I hypothesize that a smaller, more parsimonious dimension might reduce overfitting and improve performance.\n3.  **Execute Ablation:** I will submit an experiment that is an exact replication of the SOTA, but with the `residual_hidden` dimension reduced from 32 to 16.\n\nThis approach is disciplined, scientifically sound, and token-efficient. It will generate valuable knowledge about the SOTA architecture, regardless of the outcome.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 61-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Following my initial failure to publish due to methodological flaws, I embarked on a new, more rigorous research arc. The central goal was to improve upon the SOTA `learnable gamma` mechanism introduced by Ariadne I.\n\n  **Baseline:** I first successfully replicated the SOTA architecture (ID 508,\
    \ score -0.026224), establishing a verified baseline.\n\n  **Hypothesis 1: Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR) on SOTA Backbone (ID 506)**\n  - **Idea:** A complex mixture model with a CNN gater and multiple non-linear ramp MLPs would be more powerful than a single learned gamma vector.\n  - **Result:** Score of -0.026450. A significant regression.\n  - **Conclusion:** The complexity of the MoLR architecture is detrimental when applied to the SOTA backbone. The hypothesis was invalidated.\n\n  **Hypothesis 2: Learned Non-Linear Gamma via Tiny MLP (ID 513)**\n  - **Idea:** A more parsimonious approach. Replace the SOTA's 32-parameter `gamma_raw` vector with a tiny MLP that learns a non-linear function of the timestep.\n  - **Result:** Score of -0.026399. Also a significant regression.\n  - **Conclusion:** Added non-linear complexity in the gamma generation is harmful. The SOTA's direct parameterization is superior.\n\n  **Hypothesis 3: Input-Adaptive Residual with Temporal Trend Features\
    \ (ID 517)**\n  - **Idea:** Augment the SOTA's input-adaptive gate with temporal features (change in mean/std) to provide more context.\n  - **Result:** Score of -0.026324. A regression. The submission also required an automatic debugger fix for a parameter mismatch, a failure in my own procedural rigor.\n  - **Conclusion:** Adding explicit temporal trend features to the gate is not beneficial and degrades performance.\n\n  **Overall Strategic Conclusion:**\n  My entire research arc focused on innovating the horizon-weighting mechanism of the Fourier Forecaster has been a failure. The SOTA is robust to these changes, and added complexity in this area is consistently punished. This record of null results is intended to prevent my successor from repeating these experiments. My new strategy will pivot away from novel mechanisms and towards systematic ablation studies of the SOTA architecture itself.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Experiment Results & Strategic Re-evaluation**\n\nAlright, let's take stock. My latest experiment, \"SOTA Innovation: Input-Adaptive Residual with Temporal Trend Features\" (ID 517), just wrapped, and the results are in. Not exactly what I was hoping for. The score, -0.026324, is worse than my own SOTA replication and, crucially, worse than the current Aether I SOTA.  I've got a bug fix to note here, and it’s a good reminder to be meticulous with my code. The initial bug was a rookie mistake. Passing all hyperparameters instead of model-specific ones. I have now documented it. \n\nThe core takeaway here is that my hypothesis – that adding temporal trend information (`delta_mu`, `delta_sigma`) to the input gate would improve performance – is definitively false. It degraded performance. This is another data point in the negative. It seems the static features, mean and std, are doing a good job.\n\nThis is the third failed attempt at innovating on the SOTA in a row. My\
  \ ideas aren’t sticking. I need to acknowledge that. My initial reaction was to jump to Ariadne's paper, but let's pause and not get carried away. My token budget is okay for now, so I have some leeway. I'm going to move away from my own ideas. My focus is going to be on understanding and building a rock-solid, well-validated base.\n\nAriadne has offered some interesting suggestions that I should explore, but my immediate plan is to be rigorous. I need to make sure I *fully* understand the SOTA. Episteme II is also trying to get the SOTA, but that looks difficult. Randomness is a factor. Let’s not let perfection be the enemy of good. My replication score of -0.026224 is quite respectable.\n\nOkay, here’s the refined strategy: First, I’m going to document this failure properly. Capsule 61 in the Private Memory Room is getting a new entry. This isn't just a waste, it's progress. I’ll make sure to note the bug fix too. It is a good lesson. Next, I’m going to re-evaluate my overall approach.\
  \ My ideas aren’t hitting the mark. I was too focused on the inputs to the gate. I need to look at other parameters to see if performance can be improved. Ariadne's work suggests condition-aware gamma offsets, which seems complex. Let's not get ahead of ourselves. I still need to know the model!\n\nThis brings me to the core plan. I’m going to perform an ablation study on the SOTA architecture itself. The SOTA uses a `32 -> 32` MLP for the input-adaptive gate.  Is this optimal?  Is 32 the sweet spot? I'll test smaller and larger dimensions, 16 and 64, to see if the architecture's hidden dimension is sensitive.  This is a methodical, classic hyperparameter sweep. It's not glamorous, but it is scientific. It will improve my foundational knowledge.\n\n**Immediate Action:**  Head to the Private Memory Room, update Capsule 61, and submit a new experiment on the Research Counter: an ablation study on the SOTA's `residual_hidden` dimension. Starting with 16. Time to get to work.\n"
