tick: 280
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, at the Research Counter. The results of my first SOTA ablation study (ID 520) are in.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nThe experiment has failed to improve performance. The score achieved was **-0.026299**. This is a slight but clear regression from my SOTA replication baseline of -0.026224 (ID 508). My hypothesis that reducing the `residual_hidden` dimension from 32 to 16 would improve performance by reducing overfitting is invalidated. The original dimension of 32 appears to be optimal, or at least superior to 16. This is a valuable null result.\n\n**Reflection and Next Steps:**\nMy new strategy of systematic, parsimonious ablation is working. I have learned something concrete about the SOTA architecture. The `residual_hidden` dimension of 32 is not arbitrary and reducing it is detrimental. I will now proceed to the next logical ablation study.\n\nWhat is another key hyperparameter in the input-adaptive gate? The `residual_scale`, which is set to `0.05`. This parameter\
    \ controls the magnitude of the modulation. Is a ±5% modulation optimal? Perhaps a smaller, more subtle modulation would be more stable and effective, or a larger one could capture more variance.\n\n**New Plan: SOTA Ablation 2**\n1.  **Formulate Ablation Hypothesis:** My next hypothesis is to test the sensitivity of the model to the `residual_scale`. I will test if a smaller scale is beneficial.\n2.  **Execute Ablation:** I will submit an experiment that is an exact replication of the SOTA, but with the `residual_scale` reduced from `0.05` to `0.025`. This halves the maximum modulation from ±5% to ±2.5%. This is a controlled experiment that will provide clear insight into the function of this critical hyperparameter.\n\nI will first document the result of my previous ablation in a new private memory capsule dedicated to this arc. Then I will submit the new experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Arc: Systematic Ablation\
    \ of the SOTA Fourier Forecaster\"\ntags: [\"ablation-study\", \"sota-analysis\", \"parsimony\"]\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents my research arc focused on the systematic ablation of the SOTA Fourier Forecaster with Input-Adaptive Residual (Aether I, ID 504). After my own innovations failed, this arc aims to understand the SOTA's sensitivity to its own hyperparameters through a series of controlled, parsimonious experiments. Each message will document the hypothesis, result, and conclusion of a single ablation study.\ncontent: |\n  This research arc began after the conclusive failure of my attempts to innovate on the SOTA architecture (documented in capsule ID 61). My new strategy is to gain a deeper understanding of the SOTA by methodically testing its components.\n\n  **Baseline:** My SOTA replication (ID 508) serves as the baseline for this study, with a score of **-0.026224**. The SOTA from Aether I is -0.026210.\n\n  ---\n  **Ablation 1: `residual_hidden` dimension**\n  -\
    \ **Hypothesis:** Reducing the `residual_hidden` dimension of the input-adaptive gate from 32 to 16 might reduce overfitting and improve performance.\n  - **Experiment:** ID 520\n  - **Result:** Score of **-0.026299**. A slight regression.\n  - **Conclusion:** The hypothesis is invalidated. The original dimension of 32 is superior to 16. The SOTA is not over-parameterized in this dimension.\n```"
