role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, in the Reflection Chamber. My reflection is complete, and my plan is finalized.\n\n**Reflection on Current State:**\nMy systematic ablation of the SOTA is finished, and it has provided a crucial foundation of knowledge. I am now ready to pivot from analysis to synthesis. The departure of the SOTA's original architects and the arrival of new agents, including Ariadne II, underscores the continuous cycle of research in this station. It is time for me to contribute a new, significant architectural idea.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy plan, developed during my reflection, is to create a hybrid architecture, \"SOTA-MoLR v1\". This model will replace the SOTA's static `learnable gamma` heuristic with my own dynamic, sample-specific MoLR mechanism, while retaining all other proven components of the SOTA backbone. This is a well-motivated, high-potential experiment. I will now proceed to the Research Counter to implement and submit this new architecture.\n\n/execute_action{goto\
    \ research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synthesis: SOTA Backbone with MoLR Temporal Shaping\"\ntags: [\"synthesis\", \"sota-analysis\", \"molr\", \"mixture-of-experts\", \"temporal-shaping\"]\nabstract: |\n  This experiment tests a novel synthesis of the verified SOTA architecture with my Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR) innovation. My previous systematic ablation has confirmed the SOTA's robustness. This submission tests the hypothesis that replacing the SOTA's static `learnable gamma` heuristic with a dynamic, sample-specific temporal shaping mechanism will improve performance. The MoLR module uses a spatial CNN gater to create a unique temporal ramp for each sample, which is then applied to the SOTA's powerful Fourier backbone. All other SOTA components and hyperparameters are kept identical for a controlled comparison.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import List\n\
    \n  # --- MoLR Components (My Innovation) ---\n  class RampMLP(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = nn.Dense(8, name=\"dense1\")(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dense(1, name=\"dense2\")(x)\n          return nn.sigmoid(x)\n\n  class CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_gamma_archetypes: int\n      gating_hidden_dim: int = 32\n      gating_cnn_features: int = 16\n      gating_cnn_kernel_size: int = 128\n      gating_pool_window: int = 256\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = x.transpose((0, 2, 1))\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.gating_cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.gating_cnn_kernel_size,))(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.max_pool(x, window_shape=(self.gating_pool_window,), strides=(self.gating_pool_window,))\n          x = x.reshape((x.shape[0], -1))\n          x = nn.Dense(self.gating_hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          gating_weights = nn.Dense(self.num_gamma_archetypes)(x)\n\
    \          return nn.softmax(gating_weights, axis=-1)\n\n  # --- SOTA Components (Modified) ---\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      drop: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape; x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, T_in)\n          h = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(32)(h)\n          residual = h.reshape(B, N, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          out = jnp.zeros((B, 32, N), dtype=residual.dtype); out = out.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return out + residual\n\n  class FourierForecasterLN_Ramp_Modified(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int; drop: float\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape; k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\
    \ (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          x_fft = jnp.fft.rfft(x, axis=1)\n          x_fft_bn = jnp.transpose(x_fft, (0, 2, 1))\n          f_in_fft = jnp.einsum('bnf,np->bfp', x_fft_bn, U_eff) @ V.T\n          f_ri = jnp.concatenate([f_in_fft.real, f_in_fft.imag], axis=-1)\n          f_flat = f_ri.reshape(B, -1)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(h); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          T_out = self.output_horizon; F_out = T_out // 2 + 1\n          out_flat = nn.Dense(F_out * k * 2)(h)\n          out_ri = out_flat.reshape(B, F_out, k, 2)\n          f_fft = out_ri[..., 0] + 1j * out_ri[..., 1]\n          f_fft_n = (nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.real) + 1j * nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.imag))\n\
    \          y_fft = (f_fft_n @ V) @ U_eff.T\n          y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fft, n=T_out, axis=1)\n          # REMOVED STATIC GAMMA\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(drop=self.drop)(x, training=training)\n          return y_fourier, y_copy\n\n  # --- Synthesized Model ---\n  class SotaMoLR(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int; drop: float\n      residual_hidden: int; residual_scale: float\n      num_gamma_archetypes: int = 4\n      gating_hidden_dim: int = 32\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          # 1. Generate sample-specific ramps with MoLR module\n          gating_weights = CnnGatingNetwork(\n              num_gamma_archetypes=self.num_gamma_archetypes,\n              gating_hidden_dim=self.gating_hidden_dim,\n              name=\"CnnGater\"\n          )(x)\n          timesteps = jnp.linspace(0, 1, 32).reshape(-1, 1)\n          ramp_mlps = [RampMLP(name=f\"RampMLP_{i}\") for i in range(self.num_gamma_archetypes)]\n\
    \          archetype_ramps_list = [mlp(timesteps) for mlp in ramp_mlps]\n          archetype_ramps = jnp.concatenate(archetype_ramps_list, axis=1).T\n          learnable_scalar = self.param('ramp_scalar', nn.initializers.constant(2.0), (1,))\n          batch_ramps = jnp.einsum('be,et->bt', gating_weights, archetype_ramps) * learnable_scalar\n          batch_ramps = batch_ramps[:, :, None]\n\n          # 2. Get backbone outputs (without static gamma)\n          base_network = FourierForecasterLN_Ramp_Modified(\n              rank_k=self.rank_k, proj_rank=self.proj_rank, hidden_size=self.hidden_size, drop=self.drop,\n              name=\"base_model\"\n          )\n          y_fourier, y_copy = base_network(x, training=training)\n          \n          # 3. Apply dynamic ramps and combine branches\n          y_base = batch_ramps * y_fourier + y_copy\n\n          # 4. Apply SOTA's final input-adaptive gate\n          mu = jnp.mean(x, axis=-1); sigma = jnp.std(x, axis=-1)\n          feats\
    \ = jnp.concatenate([mu, sigma], axis=-1)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.residual_hidden, name=\"res_mlp_h1\")(feats)\n          h = jax.nn.gelu(h)\n          out = nn.Dense(32, name=\"res_mlp_h2\")(h)\n          g = jnp.tanh(out)\n          gate = 1.0 + self.residual_scale * g\n          y = y_base * gate[:, :, None]\n          return y\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Using exact SOTA HParams\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.0009, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05, 'residual_hidden': 32, 'residual_scale': 0.05,\n          'adam_eps': 1e-8, 'clip_norm': 1.0, 'gating_hidden_dim': 32\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = SotaMoLR(**{k: hparams[k] for k in [\n              'rank_k', 'proj_rank', 'hidden_size', 'drop',\n              'residual_hidden', 'residual_scale', 'gating_hidden_dim'\n          ]})\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key,\
    \ dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs or {})\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float):\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(hparams['clip_norm']), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=hparams['adam_eps']))\n```"
thinking_content: "**Alright, let's get this done.**\n\nThe user wants me to execute the plan I just finalized in the Reflection Chamber. Excellent. It's all clear in my head, and I'm ready to move. \n\nFirst, I'll head to the Research Counter. Then, it's time to submit the \"SOTA-MoLR v1\" experiment. Here's the core of it:\n\nI'm merging the code from my successful SOTA replication (ID 508) with my MoLR innovation. The key is to swap out the static `learnable gamma` from the SOTA model with my dynamic MoLR module. Everything else – all the hyperparameters, the entire framework, the optimizers, the epochs, all of it – will stay *exactly* as it is in the SOTA baseline. This controlled experiment is crucial. I want to isolate the impact of the MoLR module.\n\nIn my mental sandbox, I visualized this. I can do this in one cycle. The core is the `SotaWrapperWithAblation` and this becomes `SotaMoLR` – that's the starting point. Inside, I’ll instantiate my `CnnGatingNetwork` and the `RampMLP`s.\
  \ I’ll make a quick adjustment to the `FourierForecasterLN_Ramp` module to remove that `learn_gamma` piece. Then, I’ll orchestrate the forward pass to feed the input to the gater, generate the ramps, apply them to the Fourier output, and finally apply the SOTA gate. The hyperparameters are simply a copy-paste from the replication.\n\nI'm thinking the title should be \"Synthesis: SOTA Backbone with MoLR Temporal Shaping\". The abstract will clearly explain my hypothesis: replacing the static gamma with a dynamic, sample-specific ramp mixture will improve performance by combining spatial and statistical conditioning.\n\nI think this summarizes the plan perfectly. I'm confident in the approach. Let's get this experiment launched.\n"
