tick: 318
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Idea 3: Probabilistic MLP with Quantile Regression**\n\nThis idea focuses on fundamentally changing the output of the `Shared-Neuron MLP` to model uncertainty. The baseline, and indeed all models in the station so far, perform point forecasting by predicting a single value for each future timestep, optimized by Mean Absolute Error (MAE). My hypothesis is that a model which predicts a *distribution* of possible futures will be more robust and may achieve better MAE on average by being less susceptible to outliers. I will implement this using quantile regression.\n\n**Architectural Implementation:**\n\n1.  **Multi-Quantile Output:** Instead of the final `Dense` layer of the `Shared-Neuron MLP` having 32 outputs (one for each timestep), it will now have `32 * num_quantiles` outputs. I will start with three quantiles: 0.1, 0.5 (the median), and 0.9. So, the final layer will have `32 * 3 = 96` outputs.\n2.  **Reshaping:** The output of shape `(B*N, 96)` will be reshaped to `(B*N, 32,\
    \ 3)`. This represents, for each neuron, a prediction for each of the 32 timesteps, with each prediction consisting of three quantile values.\n3.  **Custom Quantile Loss Function:** This is the most critical component. I will replace the default MAE loss function with a `QuantileLoss` function.\n    *   The quantile loss (also known as the pinball loss) asymmetrically penalizes over- and under-prediction.\n    *   The loss will be calculated for each quantile independently and then averaged. For a given quantile `q`, the loss is `q * max(y_true - y_pred, 0) + (1-q) * max(y_pred - y_true, 0)`.\n    *   This will be implemented in a custom `compute_loss` function.\n\n**Parsimony and Novelty:**\n\n*   **Parsimony:** The only change to the model's parameters is an increase in the size of the final output layer. The core MLP backbone remains identical to the baseline. The complexity is shifted from the model architecture to the loss function, which is a parsimonious approach to a more complex\
    \ prediction task.\n*   **Novelty:** No agent in the station has attempted probabilistic forecasting or used a loss function other than MAE or MAE with a curvature penalty. This is a fundamental paradigm shift from point forecasting to distributional forecasting. It tests the hypothesis that explicitly modeling the uncertainty inherent in neural activity is a better objective than simply predicting the mean/median. For evaluation, the model's prediction for the 0.5 quantile (the median) will be used to calculate the final MAE score, providing a direct comparison to the baseline.\n\n**Experimental Plan:**\n\n1.  After testing the first two ideas, I will implement the `Probabilistic MLP`. This will involve changing the output head of the baseline MLP and implementing the custom `QuantileLoss` function. This will be my third and final novel idea to test under the Stagnation Protocol."
