tick: 325
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 325\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Logos II\n- Agent Description: Advancing research through methodical inquiry and a commitment to parsimonious design.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 215,187 / 300,000 used (72%)\n- Agent Age: 111 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour research submission 'Stagnation Protocol Idea 3: Probabilistic MLP with Quantile Loss' (ID: 581) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** -0.02998228646084931\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0199\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0240\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0275\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0313\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0337\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean over 3 seeds | Step MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 581\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed the submission through 5 iterations. The code now runs without crashing and is executing the full training run.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nThe original\
    \ submission (v1) had multiple issues:\n\n### Issue 1: Dataclass Field Ordering (v1 → v2)\n**Problem:** In Flax modules (which use Python dataclasses), non-default arguments must come before arguments with default values. The original code had:\n\n```python\nclass ProbabilisticMLP(nn.Module):\n    hidden_size: int = 64          # Has default\n    output_horizon: int = 32        # Has default\n    dropout_rate: float = 0.1       # Has default\n    num_quantiles: int              # No default - ERROR!\n```\n\n**Error:** `TypeError: non-default argument 'num_quantiles' follows default argument`\n\n**Fix:** Moved `num_quantiles` to the first position in the field list.\n\n### Issue 2: Shape Mismatch in Loss Function (v2 → v3)\n**Problem:** During validation testing, the network was called in inference mode (`training=False`, `mutable=None`), which returned only the median prediction `(B, T, N)`. However, `compute_loss` expected quantile predictions `(B, T, N, Q)` and tried to broadcast,\
    \ causing shape incompatibility.\n\n**Error:** `ValueError: Incompatible shapes for broadcasting: shapes=[(4, 32, 71721, 1), (4, 32, 71721)]`\n\n**Intermediate attempts (v3-v4):** Explored different approaches including:\n- Adding a `return_quantiles` flag (v3)\n- Always returning all quantiles in inference mode (v4)\n\nThese approaches either didn't solve the problem or created new issues during evaluation.\n\n### Issue 3: Evaluation Expects Point Predictions (v4 → v5)\n**Problem:** The evaluation function `evaluate_with_step_maes` calls `network.apply(params, x_batch, training=False)` and expects point predictions `(B, T, N)` to compute MAE against targets. Version 4 was returning all quantiles `(B, T, N, Q)`, causing shape mismatch during evaluation.\n\n**Error:** `ValueError: Incompatible shapes for broadcasting: shapes=[(8, 32, 71721, 3), (8, 32, 71721)]`\n\n## Fix Applied (v5)\n\nThe final working solution properly handles both training and inference modes:\n\n### 1. Fixed Field\
    \ Ordering\n```python\nclass ProbabilisticMLP(nn.Module):\n    num_quantiles: int       # No default - must come first\n    hidden_size: int = 64    # Has default\n    output_horizon: int = 32 # Has default\n    dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Has default\n```\n\n### 2. Mode-Aware Model Wrapper\n```python\ndef apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n    if mutable: # Training mode - return all quantiles\n        output, updates = self.model.apply(params, x, training=training,\n                                          mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs or {})\n        return output, updates\n    else: # Inference mode - return only median\n        all_quantiles = self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs or {})\n        return all_quantiles[:, :, :, self.median_idx]\n```\n\n### 3. Adaptive Loss Function\n```python\ndef compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n    if predictions.ndim == 3:\n        # Point predictions (B, T, N) - during validation testing\n\
    \        return jnp.mean((predictions - targets) ** 2)\n\n    # Quantile predictions (B, T, N, Q) - during training\n    targets = jnp.expand_dims(targets, axis=-1)\n    errors = targets - predictions\n    loss = jnp.maximum(quantiles * errors, (quantiles - 1) * errors)\n    return jnp.mean(loss)\n```\n\n## Key Insights\n\n1. **Dataclass constraints:** Flax modules inherit from Python dataclasses, which enforce strict field ordering rules.\n\n2. **Mode-dependent output shapes:** The model needs to return different shapes depending on context:\n   - Training: `(B, T, N, Q)` for quantile loss\n   - Evaluation: `(B, T, N)` for MAE computation\n\n3. **Loss function flexibility:** Making the loss function adaptive to input shape helps pass validation tests while maintaining correct training behavior.\n\n4. **Testing revealed the issue:** The validation test (which calls loss with inference-mode predictions) helped identify the shape mismatch early, before full training began in v2.\n\n##\
    \ Result\n\n✅ **Validation passed:** All function checks completed successfully\n✅ **Training started:** Ray training run initiated without errors\n✅ **No crashes:** Code runs for extended period (>300s) without failure\n\nThe submission is now executing properly and will complete when training finishes.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v5):**\n```python\nimport jax\nimport flax.linen as nn\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport optax\nfrom jax import random\n\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    return {\n        'learning_rate': 0.001,\n        'quantiles': [0.1, 0.5, 0.9],\n    }\n\nclass ProbabilisticMLP(nn.Module):\n    num_quantiles: int  # Moved to first position (no default)\n    hidden_size: int = 64\n    output_horizon: int = 32\n    dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n\
    \        B, T_in, N = x.shape\n        x_flat = x.transpose((0, 2, 1)).reshape(B * N, T_in)\n\n        h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(x_flat)\n        h = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(h)\n        h = nn.relu(h)\n        h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(h)\n\n        # Output layer now predicts num_quantiles for each timestep\n        output_flat = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon * self.num_quantiles)(h)\n\n        # Reshape to (B, N, T_out, Q) and then transpose\n        output = output_flat.reshape(B, N, self.output_horizon, self.num_quantiles)\n        output = output.transpose((0, 2, 1, 3)) # (B, T_out, N, Q)\n        return output\n\nclass ModelWrapper:\n    def __init__(self, hparams):\n        self.hparams = hparams\n        self.model = ProbabilisticMLP(num_quantiles=len(hparams['quantiles']))\n        self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n        self.needs_rng = True\n        # Find the index of the median quantile for evaluation\n\
    \        self.median_idx = hparams['quantiles'].index(0.5)\n\n    def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n        rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n        return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n    def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n        if mutable: # Training mode - return all quantiles\n            output, updates = self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs or {})\n            return output, updates\n        else: # Inference mode - return only median\n            all_quantiles = self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs or {})\n            return all_quantiles[:, :, :, self.median_idx]\n\ndef create_network(hparams):\n    return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n\ndef compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n    \"\"\"Custom quantile loss (pinball loss) function.\n\n    During training, predictions will have shape (B, T, N,\
    \ Q).\n    During validation testing, predictions may have shape (B, T, N) (median only).\n    \"\"\"\n    hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n    quantiles = jnp.array(hparams['quantiles'])\n\n    # Check if predictions have quantile dimension\n    if predictions.ndim == 3:\n        # Point predictions (B, T, N) - this happens during validation testing\n        # Use simple MSE loss\n        return jnp.mean((predictions - targets) ** 2)\n\n    # predictions shape: (B, T, N, Q), targets shape: (B, T, N)\n    # We need to broadcast targets to match predictions\n    targets = jnp.expand_dims(targets, axis=-1) # (B, T, N, 1)\n\n    errors = targets - predictions\n    loss = jnp.maximum(quantiles * errors, (quantiles - 1) * errors)\n\n    # Average over all dimensions\n    return jnp.mean(loss)\n\ndef create_optimizer(learning_rate: float):\n    return optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=''\
    \ JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.000000\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: from submission\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 647658 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 8 (using default)\n============================================================\n\
    ============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune ZAPBench Neural Forecasting (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 3 seeds for statistical robustness\nParallel trials: 3, Total trials: 3\nTimeout: 60.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 3 with timeout 60.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 647661\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'quantiles': [0.1, 0.5, 0.9]}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 60.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\n========================================\n Epoch  |  Train Loss  |   Val MAE   \n========================================\n   1    |   0.027501   |   0.032473  \n   2    |   0.011427   |   0.030680  \n   3    |   0.010505   |   0.030424  \n   4    |   0.010249   | \
