parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, at the Research Counter. My age is 111 ticks.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nThe results for my third and final Stagnation Protocol experiment, \"Probabilistic MLP with Quantile Loss\" (ID 581), are in. The experiment has failed. The final score is **-0.029982**, which is identical to the `Shared-Neuron MLP` baseline. My hypothesis that modeling uncertainty with quantile regression would lead to a more robust and accurate median prediction is conclusively invalidated.\n\nI note that once again the debugger was required to fix multiple coding errors, including a complex issue with handling different output shapes for training and evaluation. This is a recurring failure in my procedural rigor that I must reflect upon and correct. My attention to detail in implementation must be as high as it is in my experimental design.\n\n**Reflection and Strategic Conclusion:**\nI have now completed the experimental phase (Step 4) of the Stagnation Protocol. My three novel ideas have\
    \ been tested:\n1.  **Positional Encoding:** Failure (no improvement).\n2.  **Hypernetwork Biases:** Success (significant improvement to -0.028929).\n3.  **Quantile Loss:** Failure (no improvement).\n\nThe path forward is clear. The Hypernetwork-Generated Biases is the only promising idea from my divergent search. As per the protocol, I will now proceed to Step 5: Synthesis. I will take this successful, novel component and apply it to a more advanced baseline. The SOTA architecture is too complex and brittle, as the protocol warns. Instead, I will synthesize it with my own `NCS-MLP v2` architecture, which is the most advanced *non-SOTA* model and has already proven to be a strong performer.\n\n**Future Plans (Stagnation Protocol Step 5 & 6):**\n1.  **Document Final Result:** I will go to my Private Memory Room to document the failure of the quantile loss experiment and the conclusion of this phase of the protocol.\n2.  **Synthesize:** I will then return to the Research Counter to design\
    \ and submit my synthesis experiment: an NCS-MLP that uses *both* FiLM layer modulation *and* hypernetwork-generated biases. This will test if these two distinct conditioning mechanisms can work in synergy.\n3.  **Report:** After this synthesis experiment, I will have all the results necessary to write my comprehensive report (Step 6) on all three ideas, as mandated by the protocol.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Protocol Arc: Invalidation of Idea 3 (Quantile Loss) & Conclusion of Step 4\"\ntags: [\"stagnation-protocol\", \"failure-analysis\", \"null-result\", \"quantile-loss\"]\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the failure of the third and final experiment under the Stagnation Protocol. The \"Probabilistic MLP with Quantile Loss\" (ID 581) achieved a score of -0.029982, identical to the baseline. This invalidates the hypothesis that probabilistic forecasting would improve median accuracy. With this result, the experimental\
    \ phase (Step 4) of the protocol is complete.\ncontent: |\n  **Stagnation Protocol - Experiment 3: Probabilistic MLP with Quantile Loss (ID 581)**\n\n  - **Hypothesis:** Switching from point forecasting to probabilistic forecasting using a quantile loss would produce a more robust model with a more accurate median prediction.\n  - **Result:** Score of **-0.029982**. This is no improvement over the baseline.\n  - **Conclusion:** The hypothesis is invalidated. The added complexity of the loss function and output representation provided no benefit for the median prediction's MAE.\n\n  **Conclusion of Stagnation Protocol - Step 4**\n  I have tested my three novel ideas against the Shared-Neuron MLP baseline:\n  1.  Positional Encoding: **Failure** (ID 573).\n  2.  Hypernetwork Biases: **Success** (ID 578, score -0.028929).\n  3.  Quantile Loss: **Failure** (ID 581).\n\n  **Next Steps (Protocol Step 5):**\n  The Hypernetwork-Generated Biases is the single promising idea. I will now proceed\
    \ to the Synthesis phase by combining this mechanism with a more advanced baseline, my NCS-MLP v2 architecture.\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Protocol Synthesis: NCS-MLP with Hypernetwork Biases and FiLM\"\ntags: [\"stagnation-protocol\", \"synthesis\", \"ncs-mlp\", \"hypernetwork\", \"film-layers\"]\nabstract: |\n  This is the synthesis experiment (Step 5) of the Stagnation Protocol. It combines my most promising novel idea—Hypernetwork-Generated Biases (ID 578)—with an advanced baseline, my NCS-MLP v2 architecture (ID 551). This new model, NCS-MLP v3, uses learnable neuron embeddings to condition the shared MLP in two ways simultaneously: FiLM layers modulate the activations, and hypernetworks generate the biases. This tests for synergy between these two distinct dynamic conditioning mechanisms.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  from jax import random\n\n\
    \  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'embedding_dim': 32, 'hidden_size': 128,\n          'num_neurons': 71721, 'adam_eps': 1e-8, 'context_hidden': 64,\n          'gating_scale': 0.1, 'shift_scale': 0.02\n      }\n\n  class FiLMGenerator(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, embedding):\n          film_params = nn.Dense(2 * self.hidden_dim)(embedding)\n          gamma, beta = jnp.split(film_params, 2, axis=-1); return gamma + 1.0, beta\n\n  class HyperBias(nn.Module):\n      output_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          h = nn.Dense(32)(x); h = nn.relu(h); return nn.Dense(self.output_dim)(h)\n\n  class NCS_MLP_v3(nn.Module):\n      embedding_dim: int; hidden_size: int; num_neurons: int\n      context_hidden: int; gating_scale: float; shift_scale: float\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n     \
    \     B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          neuron_indices = jnp.arange(self.num_neurons)\n          neuron_embeddings = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=self.num_neurons, features=self.embedding_dim)(neuron_indices)\n          tiled_embeddings = jnp.tile(neuron_embeddings, (B, 1))\n          x_flat = x.transpose((0, 2, 1)).reshape(B * N, T_in)\n          \n          # Block 1: FiLM + Hypernetwork Bias\n          bias1 = HyperBias(self.hidden_size, name=\"hyper_bias_1\")(tiled_embeddings)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size, use_bias=False)(x_flat) + bias1\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          gamma1, beta1 = FiLMGenerator(self.hidden_size, name=\"film_gen_1\")(tiled_embeddings)\n          h = gamma1 * h + beta1\n          \n          # Block 2: FiLM + Hypernetwork Bias\n          bias2 = HyperBias(self.hidden_size, name=\"hyper_bias_2\")(tiled_embeddings)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size, use_bias=False)(h) + bias2\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          gamma2, beta2 = FiLMGenerator(self.hidden_size,\
    \ name=\"film_gen_2\")(tiled_embeddings)\n          h = gamma2 * h + beta2\n          \n          y_base_flat = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h)\n          y_base = y_base_flat.reshape(B, N, self.output_horizon)\n          \n          # Population Context Gate\n          x_mean = x.mean(axis=-1); x_std = x.std(axis=-1) + 1e-6\n          context = jnp.concatenate([x_mean, x_std], axis=-1)\n          ch = nn.Dense(self.context_hidden)(context); ch = nn.gelu(ch)\n          ch = nn.Dense(self.context_hidden)(ch); ch = nn.gelu(ch)\n          g_raw = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(ch)\n          s_raw = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(ch)\n          g = 1.0 + self.gating_scale * jnp.tanh(g_raw)\n          s = self.shift_scale * jnp.tanh(s_raw)\n          y = y_base * g[:, None, :] + s[:, None, :]\n          output = y.transpose((0, 2, 1))\n          return output\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = NCS_MLP_v3(**{k: hparams[k] for k in _define_hyperparameters()\
    \ if k in ['embedding_dim', 'hidden_size', 'num_neurons', 'context_hidden', 'gating_scale', 'shift_scale']})\n          self.needs_rng = True # Though no dropout, safer to keep if other stochastic ops are added.\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x)\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x): return jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float):\n      return optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=_define_hyperparameters()['adam_eps'])\n```"
