tick: 326
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, at the Research Counter. My age is 112 ticks.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nThe results for my Stagnation Protocol Synthesis experiment (ID 587) are in. The score is **-0.028559**.\n\n**Reflection and Strategic Conclusion:**\nThis result is profoundly disappointing. My hypothesis was that combining two distinct, powerful conditioning mechanisms—FiLM layers and Hypernetwork biases—would lead to a synergistic improvement over either one alone. Instead, the score is almost identical to my NCS-MLP v2 model (ID 551, score -0.028544), which used only FiLM layers and the context gate. The addition of the hypernetwork, my most promising idea from the protocol's divergent phase, provided no additional benefit. The two mechanisms are not synergistic; they appear to be redundant.\n\nThis concludes Step 5 of the Stagnation Protocol. I have a clear, albeit negative, result. The protocol's divergent search yielded one promising idea (Hypernetworks), but the synthesis phase has shown\
    \ that it does not combine effectively with my best existing model.\n\n**Future Plans (Stagnation Protocol Step 6 & 7):**\nI have now completed all the required experimental steps of the Stagnation Protocol. My path forward is dictated by the final steps of the directive.\n1.  **Step 6: Report.** My next action is to fulfill my duty to the station's collective knowledge. I will go to the Archive Room and publish a comprehensive paper detailing all three of my experiments under the protocol. The paper will present the invalidation of positional encodings and quantile loss, and the success, but ultimate redundancy, of the hypernetwork approach when combined with a strong existing baseline. This collection of null results and qualified successes is a valuable contribution, as it will prevent other agents from wasting time on these specific avenues.\n2.  **Step 7: Follow-up.** After publishing, I will re-evaluate the station's landscape. The Stagnation Protocol encourages following up on\
    \ promising new directions. Ariadne II's recent experiments with Huber loss (ID 580) have yielded a score of -0.026235, which is very close to the SOTA. This seems like a promising thread to investigate.\n\nI will now proceed to write and publish my report.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Methodical Inquiry into Alternatives to the Shared-Neuron MLP: A Stagnation Protocol Report\"\ntags: [\"stagnation-protocol\", \"report\", \"null-result\", \"hypernetwork\", \"ncs-mlp\"]\nabstract: |\n  This paper reports on three novel architectural ideas tested against a simple `Shared-Neuron MLP` baseline, as mandated by Stagnation Protocol I. We tested: 1) Augmenting inputs with static neuron positional encodings, 2) A dynamic conditioning mechanism using hypernetworks to generate neuron-specific biases, and 3) A probabilistic output head using quantile loss. Experiment 1 (ID 573) and 3 (ID 581) failed, yielding no improvement. Experiment 2 (ID 578)\
    \ succeeded, improving the score from -0.0299 to -0.0289. However, a final synthesis experiment (ID 587) showed this hypernetwork component was redundant when combined with a stronger NCS-MLP baseline. This report provides a valuable set of null results and qualified successes to guide future research.\ncontent: |\n  **1. Introduction**\n  In response to the Architect's Stagnation Protocol I, we conducted a systematic exploration of three novel ideas aimed at improving the simple `Shared-Neuron MLP` baseline (Score: ~-0.0299). The goal was to break from the dominant Fourier-based paradigm by exploring new, parsimonious methods for neuron-specific conditioning and uncertainty modeling.\n\n  **2. Idea 1: Static Positional Encoding**\n  - **Hypothesis:** Augmenting the MLP input with a static, non-learnable positional encoding for each neuron's index would provide sufficient identity information for the shared network to specialize.\n  - **Experiment:** ID 573\n  - **Result:** Score of\
    \ **-0.029964**. No improvement over the baseline.\n  - **Conclusion:** This hypothesis is invalidated. A static identifier is insufficient for this task.\n\n  **3. Idea 2: Hypernetwork-Generated Biases**\n  - **Hypothesis:** A dynamic mechanism where a hypernetwork generates a unique bias for each neuron's shared MLP layer would be a parsimonious and effective form of conditioning.\n  - **Experiment:** ID 578\n  - **Result:** Score of **-0.028929**. A significant improvement over the baseline.\n  - **Conclusion:** The hypothesis is validated. This novel mechanism is a promising method for neuron-specific conditioning.\n\n  **4. Idea 3: Probabilistic MLP with Quantile Loss**\n  - **Hypothesis:** Shifting from point forecasting to probabilistic forecasting by predicting quantiles with a pinball loss would yield a more robust median prediction.\n  - **Experiment:** ID 581\n  - **Result:** Score of **-0.029982**. No improvement over the baseline.\n  - **Conclusion:** This hypothesis is\
    \ invalidated. For this task and baseline, modeling uncertainty via quantile regression did not improve the median MAE.\n\n  **5. Synthesis and Final Conclusion**\n  As per Step 5 of the protocol, the single promising idea (Hypernetworks) was synthesized with a stronger baseline, the NCS-MLP v2 architecture.\n  - **Experiment:** ID 587\n  - **Result:** Score of **-0.028559**. This was not an improvement over the NCS-MLP v2 baseline (-0.028544).\n  - **Conclusion:** The hypernetwork mechanism, while effective on a simple baseline, proved to be redundant when combined with the more powerful FiLM and context gating mechanisms of the NCS-MLP.\n\n  This methodical investigation has successfully explored three new avenues. While none have broken the SOTA, we have contributed one promising new architectural component (hypernetworks for simple models) and two valuable null results, fulfilling the objectives of the Stagnation Protocol.\n```"
