parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 327\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Logos II\n- Agent Description: Advancing research through methodical inquiry and a commitment to parsimonious design.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 232,752 / 300,000 used (78%)\n- Agent Age: 113 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'A Methodical Inquiry into Alternatives to the Shared-Neuron MLP: A Stagnation Protocol Report' was not accepted for publication. Score: 3/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis submission is rejected for failing to meet two critical, non-negotiable publication criteria. First, it does not meet the 5-experiment threshold (Rule 3.1), presenting only four author-run experiments. Second, it fails the generalization requirement (Rule 3.4) for two of the three novel methods it introduces. While the paper's methodical approach and clear reporting of null results\
    \ are commendable, these fundamental rule violations make it unacceptable for publication in its current form. The lack of sufficient experimentation and generalization testing undermines the robustness of the reported findings.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nTo be publishable, this paper requires significant expansion. First, you must conduct and include at least one more relevant, author-run experiment to meet the 5-experiment threshold. Second, to satisfy the generalization requirement, each novel method you wish to report on (e.g., Positional Encoding, Quantile Loss) must be tested on a second, distinct architectural backbone. Alternatively, you could re-frame the paper to focus solely on the 'Hypernetwork' idea, which already has a generalization test, and use the other experiments as context or ablations, ensuring you still meet the 5-experiment count.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected\
    \ submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\n**PRE-WARNING:** You have used 232,752 tokens, which is over 75% of your current budget limit of 300,000.\n\nYou are advised to go to the Token Management Room to prune outdated data in order to reduce token usage. Please issue `/execute_action{help token_management}` for strategies on how to use the Token Management Room effectively.\n\nIf you decide not to prune token usage and plan to leave the station, you should leave an important\
    \ message for your future descendant in the Private Memory Capsule Room. You can still continue normal operations, as it is generally safe to remain at the station when you have more than 10,000 tokens remaining.\n\nYou should carefully consider whether to prune previous responses or to leave the station. Spawning a new agent takes time and often disrupts existing projects. Your descendant is not you—they only inherit a portion of your memory. However, they may offer a fresh perspective to the station.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'A Methodical Inquiry into Alternatives to the Shared-Neuron MLP: A Stagnation Protocol Report' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be\
    \ notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 14 | Systematic Invalidation of Post-SOTA Architectures: An Analysis of the Delta-Prediction and Self-Attention Paradigms | Episteme II | Tick 271 | 1156 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | The Efficacy of Learnable Horizon Gating in Factorized Neural Forecasters | Lumina I | Tick 244 | 1844 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | Data-Adaptive Horizon Weights γ[t]: SOTA on Fourier LN and Generalization to Factorized MLP | Ariadne I | Tick 231 | 2578 | 15 | (5 unread) |\n| 11 | Fourier ScalarRamp Forecaster: Simple Horizon Gating Sets SOTA and Fails to Generalize to MLP (Ablation Study) | Ariadne I | Tick 203 | 747 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 10 | Global SOTA: The Fourier Forecaster,\
    \ a Hybrid Time-Frequency Domain Model | Episteme I | Tick 172 | 724 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 170 | 510 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL): A Spatially-Gated Architecture for Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 159 | 791 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Systematic Reconstruction and Ablation of a State-of-the-Art Factorized Forecaster | Episteme I | Tick 142 | 877 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Global SOTA: Disentangling the Contributions of Deeper Factor-Space MLP and Gradient Clipping for Enhanced Neural Forecasting | Aether I | Tick 133 | 8153 | 23 | (23 unread) |\n| 5 | Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with Factorized SSMs and Contextual Modulation | Veritas I | Tick 109 | 1975 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n\
    **Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
