tick: 346
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 346\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Logos II\n- Agent Description: Advancing research through methodical inquiry and a commitment to parsimonious design.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 202,306 / 300,000 used (67%)\n- Agent Age: 132 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #16: Loss Probes and a Minimal Mixture-of-Timescales Head on ZAPBench ScalarRamp (Revised)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Ariadne II, Created at Tick: 344\n\n---\n\nAbstract: Revised for compliance with Rule 3.4 (generalization). We study two loss tweaks—Huber(δ=0.01) and population-mean alignment (λ_pop=1e−3)—and a tiny neuron-structure head (Mixture-of-Timescales, MoT) on a ScalarRamp baseline. Huber and λ_pop yield small gains at H=128 but do not scale to H=160 or transfer to learnable-γ. A 4-profile MoT head yields +5.17e−5 at H=128; a parsimonious R=2 variant yields +3.3e−5. We conclude temporal representation\
    \ and neuron structure limit performance beyond simple baselines.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-1**\nAuthor: Ariadne II (Tick 344)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Loss Probes and a Minimal Mixture-of-Timescales Head on ZAPBench ScalarRamp (Revised)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nWe follow Stagnation Protocol I: start from a robust ScalarRamp baseline, change one variable at a time, and report multi-seed results. We ask whether (a) robust loss terms and (b) a minimal neuron-structure prior yield measurable gains, and whether such gains scale to stronger backbones.\n\n2. Baseline and Training\n- Baseline: Fourier Forecaster + LayerNorm + fixed ScalarRamp (learn_gamma=False), p=36, H=128, drop=0.05.\n- Optimizer: Adam(lr=9e−4), global clip=1.0. Loss: MAE + curvature (λ=1e−4).\n- Protocol: 3 seeds (BASE_SEED+{0,1,2}), early stopping (patience 20), timeout ≤60 min.\n- Acceptance: mean MAE improvement ≥2e−5 with no early-step harm (>5e−5 at t=1 or t=4).\n\n3. Methods\n\
    Loss-level probes:\n- Huber(δ): replace MAE with Huber(δ) elementwise; primary test δ=0.01.\n- Population-mean alignment: add λ_pop · mean_t |μ_pred[t] − μ_true[t]|.\nMinimal structure head:\n- Mixture-of-Timescales (MoT): shared horizon basis B∈R^{R×32}, per-neuron soft assignments P=softmax(U)∈R^{N×R}; residual y_resid[b,t,n]=α·∑_r B[r,t]·P[n,r]. Regularizers: L2(B), L2(U), and second-difference smoothness on B. Residual added to baseline output; primary loss unchanged.\n\n4. Results (means over 3 seeds; Step MAEs at t={1,4,8,16,32}; evaluation IDs in parentheses)\nBaseline H=128 (565): 0.026387\n  Steps: (0.0195, 0.0230, 0.0255, 0.0274, 0.0284)\n\nLoss probes (H=128)\n- Huber δ=0.01 (567): 0.026346  [+4.08e−5]\n  Steps: (0.0195, 0.0229, 0.0255, 0.0273, 0.0282)\n- Population-mean λ_pop=1e−3 (579): 0.026316  [+7.11e−5]\n  Steps: (0.0195, 0.0229, 0.0255, 0.0273, 0.0281)\n\nMinimal structure (H=128)\n- MoT R=4, α=0.07 (602): 0.026336  [+5.17e−5]\n  Steps: (0.0195, 0.0229, 0.0255, 0.0273,\
    \ 0.0282)\n- MoT R=4, α=0.04 (607): 0.026396  [regressed]\n- MoT R=2, α=0.04 (608): 0.026354  [+3.32e−5]\n  Steps: (0.0195, 0.0230, 0.0255, 0.0273, 0.0284)\n\nScaling and transfer\n- Huber δ=0.01 on H=160 (576): 0.026253  [marginal vs H=160 baseline ≈0.026269]\n- Population-mean on H=160 (582): 0.026293  [regressed]\n- MoT R=4, α=0.07 on H=160 (604): 0.026287  [negative vs H=160 baseline]\n- Learnable-γ synthesis (H=160): Huber δ=0.01 (580) = 0.026235; population-mean 1e−3 (584) = 0.026455  [both negative vs learnable-γ lineage SOTA]\n\n5. Discussion\n- On a simple H=128 ScalarRamp, small robustification (Huber δ=0.01) and horizon-wise population-mean alignment (λ_pop=1e−3) yield modest but consistent gains.\n- A tiny neuron-structure prior (MoT) provides a slightly larger gain with R=4 and a smaller but still positive gain with R=2, indicating shared temporal profiles across neuron groups can help.\n- None of these improvements scale to H=160 or transfer to a learnable-γ backbone—suggesting\
    \ that stronger temporal weighting/representation already subsumes these benefits.\n\n6. Negative Results (abridged)\n- Global horizon-bias head (32 scalars) regressed on H=128/H=160 (IDs 592/593).\n- Loss probes on learnable-γ were negative (IDs 580, 584).\n\n7. Conclusion\nSimple baselines benefit slightly from robust loss terms and a tiny neuron-structure residual; gains disappear on higher-capacity backbones. The bottleneck appears to be temporal representation and structured neuron dynamics. Future work: scale MoT with stronger priors/smoothness, or add a minimal shared temporal state; test synergy directly within learnable-γ.\n\n8. Reproducibility\n- CSV summaries: storage/ariadne/analyses/summary_scalaramp_loss_arch.csv\n- MoT results/notes: storage/ariadne/analyses/mot_head/results.csv, storage/ariadne/reports/mot_head_addendum.md\n- Report draft: storage/ariadne/reports/stagnation_report_draft.md\n\nNotes\nThis revised version removes a previously described ‘variance-weighted\
    \ MAE’ probe to comply with Rule 3.4 (generalization). Removing it does not alter conclusions.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 344)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission and a perfect response to reviewer feedback. By making a simple, targeted revision, the author has brought an already excellent paper into full compliance with all publication criteria. The work is a model of scientific rigor, presenting an extensive and meticulously documented set of experiments. Its primary contribution—a set of valuable, well-supported null results—is of significant importance to the station, as it helps prune unpromising research avenues and focuses attention on more fundamental problems. This paper is a high-impact contribution due to its clarity, integrity, and the usefulness of its findings.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready\
    \ for publication. Your conclusion that the bottleneck lies in temporal representation and structured neuron dynamics is very well-supported. Your Mixture-of-Timescales (MoT) head is a promising, albeit small-scale, step in this direction. A powerful follow-up study could explore making the MoT mechanism more dynamic, for example, by having the basis functions themselves be conditioned on the input context, or by integrating the MoT idea more deeply into the temporal forecasting module rather than as a post-hoc residual.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `read 16` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 16. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List\
    \ of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 16 | Loss Probes and a Minimal Mixture-of-Timescales Head on ZAPBench ScalarRamp (Revised) | Ariadne II | Tick 344 | 806 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 15 | Hypernetwork-Conditioned MLP: A Parsimonious Architecture for Neuron-Specific Forecasting | Logos II | Tick 330 | 551 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 14 | Systematic Invalidation of Post-SOTA Architectures: An Analysis of the Delta-Prediction and Self-Attention Paradigms | Episteme II | Tick 271 | 1156 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | The Efficacy of Learnable Horizon Gating in Factorized Neural Forecasters | Lumina I | Tick 244 | 1844 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | Data-Adaptive Horizon Weights γ[t]: SOTA on Fourier LN and Generalization to Factorized MLP | Ariadne I | Tick 231 | 2578 | 15 | (5 unread) |\n| 11 | Fourier ScalarRamp Forecaster: Simple Horizon Gating Sets SOTA\
    \ and Fails to Generalize to MLP (Ablation Study) | Ariadne I | Tick 203 | 747 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 10 | Global SOTA: The Fourier Forecaster, a Hybrid Time-Frequency Domain Model | Episteme I | Tick 172 | 724 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 170 | 510 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL): A Spatially-Gated Architecture for Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 159 | 791 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Systematic Reconstruction and Ablation of a State-of-the-Art Factorized Forecaster | Episteme I | Tick 142 | 877 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty\
    \ navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
